{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "*The steward of plenty*\n## The Second Light\nAfter Katsík, Apollo's light chose Aetós, and the city entered its first age of plenty. Katsík had taught the survivors to sow the soil, and Aetós taught them what to do with the harvest. Under the second Lightkeeper, Lightfall stopped merely surviving and began to build.\n## Quest for the divine seeds\nAetós Along with the Desendants of the apostles took on the second holy quest, following the oracular visions bestowed upon them. The result of this divine quest was not only plentiful harvest but also solidifying positions of noble apostles in the society. \n## The Granary\nGradually after the quest fields grew heavy in Aetós's day, heavier than the people could eat. Left alone, a surplus rots, is stolen, or is squandered, so Aetós gave it shape. The Granary of Aetós rose beside the tree's roots, where the light is warmest, and it became the heart of the city's stores. Grain was measured in, guarded, and shared out in lean seasons, and the people learned that hunger did not have to return with every winter.\nThe surplus also freed hands from the plow. Weavers, smiths, and potters could now live by their trade, which laid the groundwork for the crafters who would flourish under Távros. The Granary still stands as a symbol of that abundance. Guarded by the descendants of The apostle of sight. \n## The Walls\nA city with full stores in past times would've draws hungry eyes... but today it calls upon the cursed things beyond the light that had not forgotten the taste of living.\n Aetós ordered the great wall raised around the tree and the heart of the city, in grey stone that still circles Lightfall today. It gave the people something they had not felt since the rains began: a place that was safe because they had made it so, and not only because a god had blessed it.\n## The Apostles and the Noble Houses\nBefore the walls were finished, the old lightkeeper's vision granted him third holy quest. He called forth the apostles on the quest on his behest. And so the went on a joureny up north.. to quell the called angles from the cold desert beyond the mountains. \nThose who returned were honored above all others. Aetós made them the city's first nobles, granting them land, standing, and a share in governing the harvest they had helped protect. Their descendants became the Noble Houses of Lightfall, and each house still traces its line back to that quest.\n", "category": "NPC", "frontmatter_yaml": "role: \"Second Lightkeeper\"\naffiliation: \"Hierocracy of Lightfall\"\nstatus: \"dead\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "7bca2e93-15e6-52f4-b233-0fe5eea6552a", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "aetos-ii", "status": "published", "title": "Aetós II", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Year 97, the Fifth Divine quest, under the Lightkeeper Leander IV gave birth to individuals with divine potential, that we now clall Solarii. \n\nAll solarii's are fished out of the divine pool at the centre of Askos, being brought back to life at an ideal time.. time when the solari's potential was at its zentih. You dont remember your past life, of who you were before being a Solarii. But your path before you joined the Order of Sol Invictus is yours to decide. \n## Acolyte\n### Order of the Light Ward\n*Secluded - Bureaucratic - Academic*\n\nYou walked into the Temple's healing halls and asked to be useful. Maybe you wanted to repay the care you received, or maybe the sight of the sick pulled you in. Your days ran by the dawn and dusk prayers, with the hours between spent grinding herbs, boiling bandages, and sitting with the sick. You learned that the healers keep careful records, and that people carried in from the curse of weeping are rarely leave.\n### Hearth keeper of the old gods\n*Secluded - Anarchic - Academic*\n\nYou heard Olympian devotees mentioned in passing and went looking for them, curious about gods you couldn't name but felt you should know. They were not hard to find. Their shrines sit in plain sight: a small altar in the corner of a shop, a niche at a crossroads, a hearth with a flame kept just for them. They welcomed you and asked if you would keep one. You learned the old rites: wine poured at dusk, names spoken aloud, small offerings left at the foot of a statue. Nothing about it is forbidden, but the city has opinions. Apollo's light is expected to come first, and a devotee who forgets that will feel the cooling of a neighbor's smile and the careful silence of the Temple. Most keepers simply open their evening prayers with a nod to the tree, then turn to their own gods, and no one minds. Your circle shares the work: someone tends the flame, someone teaches the old songs, and someone listens for how the district is feeling.\n## Artisan\n### Crafter's Guild Apprentice\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Creative*\n\nYou visited the Crafter's District to see whether your hands remembered anything, and a guildmaster handed you a tool. They did. You asked to be taken on and moved through apprenticeship faster than anyone thought possible. Your trade might be smithing, armoring, light-wrighting, herbalism, or alchemy. The guild, shaped under Távros, tests every apprentice and stamps every finished piece with a maker's mark. For someone with no past, that mark was a rare comfort: here you could prove who you were by what you made.\n### Light-wright's Bench\n*Secluded - Communal - Creative*\n\nYou went to the light-wrights because you were drawn to the glow. Light-wrights shape lanterns, mirrors, and lenses, and their work is closer to prayer than to trade. The light they work with is not fire. It is a fragment of Apollo's own radiance, the same warm and steady light that fell from the tree, and a light-wright is its caretaker, not its maker. Every lantern is blessed at the Temple before it leaves the bench, and every mirror carries a priest's mark of approval. A lantern for a cleansing tower may be blessed by the Temple's priests, then guarded on the road to its post. Your master taught you that light is never to be wasted, hoarded, or sold as an ordinary good. Apprentices learn to grind glass, set reflective bronze plates, and keep their hands steady while the divine light warms the metal. You spend your days in the quiet glow of the workshop, and you have noticed that your work shines brighter than it should.\n## Charlatan\n### Counterfeit Oracle\n*Urban - Anarchic - Lawless*\n\nYou discovered that people with no past are very good at pretending to see the future. On the clear days, when the clouds thin and the ring floats overhead, you set up on a street corner and read omens for coin. You told farmers about their harvests, merchants about their fortunes, and the lovelorn about whatever they wanted to hear. The Temple's weather-seers read the true divine pattern and you read only faces, but your customers don't know the difference. You've learned to watch for a tremor in someone's voice, a glance at a neighbor, a coin counted twice. The work is quiet and a little shameful, but you are good at it, and some part of you hopes you might someday be right by accident.\n### Relic Forger\n*Urban - Anarchic - Creative*\n\nYou noticed that collectors pay well for pieces from before the Age of Heroes, and that most of them never check the stonework. So you started to make your own. You carve inscriptions in half-forgotten scripts, age bronze with salts and smoke, and pass off the results as ruin finds. Your forgeries sell to nobles, scholars, and the occasional Temple priest who wants a curiosity for a shelf. You learned that forging is a craft in its own right, with tools, methods, and a fierce pride in doing it well. You also learned to avoid the Metron's appraisers and the Ruin-delvers who know the real thing. The work taught you enough about ancient craft to fool experts, and to wonder how you learned it so quickly.\n## Criminal\n### Criminal Gang\n*Urban - Communal - Lawless*\n\nYou went looking for work that asked no questions, and one night someone offered you a job. The organization is not a gang of cutpurses. It is a quiet, well-run enterprise that moves ██████ through the Granary's back doors, lends coin to the desperate at terms nobody reads, and keeps a ledger as careful as the Inquisition's. You know you were paid on the first of every month. You know you were told never to carry anything yourself. Beyond that, the details have gone soft, like a dream in the afternoon. You remember a room with ████████, a man whose face you can't recall, and a rule you were taught on your first day: ██████████. Some people say the organization has a way of making leavers forget. Others say it is only that no one wants to remember.\n### Cult Courier\n*Urban - Anarchic - Lawless*\n\nYou were curious about the whispers that the weeping is a judgment, and a stranger at a market stall offered you coin to carry a parcel across the city. You never opened one. You learned the routes: a baker's back door, a cobbler's shuttered window, a laundry line with a particular knot. The believers speak softly and mean what they say, and some of them are neighbors you have bought bread from. You never decided whether you agreed with them, and the not-knowing is what keeps you in the work.\n## Entertainer\n### Temple Chorus\n*Urban - Communal - Creative*\n\nYou were drawn to the dawn and dusk services by the singing, and one morning you found yourself joining in. The choirmaster noticed and asked you to stay. You learned the hymns, the breath control, and the quiet discipline of singing in unison with forty strangers while the sun comes up. The old melodies come out of your mouth before you can think of them, and a few of the older singers have started watching you.\n### Gymnasion Showman\n*Urban - Communal - Martial*\n\nYou went to the public halls to see what your body could do, and found that crowds gather when it does it well. You wrestle, race, and spar in the open courts, and the audience cheers on festival days and tavern nights alike. You learned showmanship: when to pull a punch, when to let the other fighter win, and how to land a throw so the whole hall gasps. Recruiters from the Emberwatch and the Order of Frostmarch watch from the benches, and you've learned to pretend not to notice.\n## Farmer\n### Channel-tenders and Orchard Keepers\n*Rural - Communal - Labor*\n\nYou left the walls for the fields, wanting space and quiet. A halfling clan agreed to take you on, and you were given a shovel, a timepiece, and a pair of pruning shears. By day you open and close the divine channels on the minute; by season you prune and graft in the groves beyond the wall. You learned that water and fruit both reward patience and punctuality. Few outsiders have worked beside the clan, and you were one of them.\n### Herd-hand\nRural - Communal - Labor\n\nYou asked for work at the edge of the fields, and a herder handed you a staff. You drive goats and sheep to pasture inside the light of the boundary towers, and you come home at dusk when the first lamps are lit. You learned to read nervous animals: the ear that twitches before a storm, the flock that goes quiet when something is moving at the edge of the dark. The work is slow and the company is mostly animals, which suited someone with a lot to think about.\n## Guard\n### Tower Sentry\n*Secluded - Bureaucratic - Martial*\n\nYou asked for a post at a cleansing tower and were given a spear, a lantern, and a shift roster. You stand watch on the boundary, guarding the flame and whoever runs toward it out of the dark. The routine is strict: hours are logged, signals are tested, and no one leaves the tower unmanned. You learned what the weeping looks like when it moves, and how still a person can become when it does.\n### City Guard\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Martial*\n\nYou took a post on the city's streets, walking a beat between the Crafter's District and the Agora. You learned the faces of the regulars, the habits of the markets, and the difference between a loud argument and a dangerous one. Most days are quiet. The work is mostly settling disputes, escorting drunks home, and being seen. You answer to the city's own watch, not the Inquisition, though you learned quickly when to step aside for them.\n## Guide\n### Pilgrim's Escort\n*Rural - Communal - Martial*\n\nYou offered your sword to the devout who walk to the boundary shrines, and a pilgrim's guild took you on. You lead small groups along the outer paths, watching for danger and keeping the pace for the slowest walker. You learned that pilgrims come for many reasons: grief, thanks, penance, doubt. You learned which of them are actually ready for the walk, and how to turn back the ones who are not.\n### Emberwatch Road-Scout\nWilderness - Communal - Martial\n\nYou joined the Emberwatch and volunteered to range ahead of the patrols. You mark safe ground, test the road, and report anything that waits in the rain. The work is lonely and a little reckless, and the scouts who last are the ones who learn to read the land before it reads them. You are trusted to come back with the truth, even when it is bad news.\n## Hermit\n### Field Hermit\n*Rural - Anarchic - Academic*\n\nYou took over a herder's hut at the edge of the fields, where the tree's light is faint and the dark is close. No one assigned it to you and no one objected. You live simply, tend a small garden, and spend your evenings watching the horizon and trying to remember. Farmers leave bread at your door and ask for no company in return. In the silence you have started to notice patterns in the dark that you cannot explain.\n### Cloistered Reader\n*Secluded - Bureaucratic - Academic*\n\nYou asked the Temple for a cell and a stack of books, and they gave you both. You spend months copying one prayer after another, waiting for something to feel familiar. The scriptorium keeps strict hours, with silence after the dusk bell and fasting on holy days. You learned the old scripts, the art of illumination, and the careful way a page is read. Most of what you copy is routine, but now and then a line catches in your mind like a hook.\n## Merchant\n### Plaza Stallkeeper\n*Urban - Communal - Creative*\n\nYou rented a stall near the Crafter's District obelisk, wanting honest work where you could watch people come and go. You sell what others make: lamps, ribbons, tools, small charms. Stallkeepers share a loose agreement about who sets up where, who minds whose goods during prayers, and who splits the cost of a bad day. You learned to haggle gently, to judge a customer in a glance, and to tell when a buyer is really looking for something else. Most of the city passes your stall eventually, and many people talk to you.\n### Grain Factor\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Labor*\n\nYou took a post as a go-between for the farms and the Granary of Aetós, wanting steady work with numbers and a view of the city's food supply. You weigh samples, negotiate prices, and settle disputes between halfling clans and granary clerks. You learned the rhythm of the harvest: which fields are heavy, which are failing, and which families are quietly short. The Granary's books must balance, and you are the person who makes them.\n## Noble\n### Noble Household Retainer\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Noble*\n\nYou applied for a post with a noble house, hoping for security and a view of how the city is run. The house hired you as an aide, curious about a Solarii with no history. You ate well, learned etiquette, and attended gatherings where the Noble Houses weigh the city's affairs. You were never a guest and never quite a servant, and you learned to read the difference in every glance.\n### Council Page\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Noble*\n\nYou carry messages for a noble house's representative at the council, running between the Agora, the Temple, the Inquisition, and the Metron. You learned the council's halls, its unwritten rules, and which doors open for a young face in house colors. Pages hear things. Nobody minds, because pages are expected to forget what they hear. You have learned how often the empty Agora comes up in conversation, and how carefully people avoid the question of who will fill it.\n## Sage\n### Philosophers' Conclave\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Academic*\n\nYou walked into the public halls because you wanted answers about the weeping, the tree, and the ring. The thinkers there argue about all three, and with no past to defend, you made an ideal debating partner. You learned to build an argument, hold a position, and let go of it gracefully. Rivalries are sharp, and your lack of a history made you oddly welcome to every faction.\n### Metron Archive Reader\n*Secluded - Bureaucratic - Academic*\n\nYou asked to study the Mage's Guild's stacks, and the Metron handed you a reader's token and a very small room. You learned how the archive is organized, which shelves are open, and which are kept behind locked doors. Readers sign for every scroll and return it by dusk. Most of what you read is dry: permit records, spell logs, old measurements. Now and then, you find something that is not.\n## Sailor\n[Details to be unlocked after Eastern Point of Light is re_established]\n## Scribe\n### Weather-seer's Recorder\n*Secluded - Bureaucratic - Academic*\n\nYou joined the oracular college as a recorder, since you wanted something to measure while you worked out who you were. Each day you log the rain's strength, the wind, and the color of the clouds, then compare them against the pattern the seers expect. Most days the pages match. You learned that the work is about patience and precision, and that a single missed entry can undo a decade of forecasts. Emberwatch captains and farmers wait on your ledgers, even if they never learn your name.\n### Inquisition Clerk\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Martial*\n\nYou took a clerk's post with the Inquisition, hoping to learn the city's laws and find out whether the pool was written down anywhere. You file the Laws of the Light, carry sealed documents between offices, and check the punch-marks on the city's coin. Every page is numbered and every seal is logged. You learned how an accusation travels from a whisper to a ruling, and that a careful clerk is trusted with more than they expect.\n### \n## Soldier\n### Emberwatch Road-company\n*Wilderness - Communal - Martial*\n\nYou enlisted with a company founded under Leander, because you wanted to see more than the city's walls. Your company trained together, ate together, and spent its days helping to light the outer roads. You hauled lamp-oil, raised posts, and set blessed lanterns at the road's edge, so that travelers would have a trail of warm light to follow. You learned that road-lighting is as much about teamwork as courage: one person holds the ladder, another trims the wick, a third watches the dark. The company became the closest thing you had to a family, and its jokes, rituals, and nicknames are the first memories you can call your own.\n### Frostmarch Recruit\n*Secluded - Bureaucratic - Martial*\n\nYou requested a place at the Frostmarch's Quarters, drawn to the hardest training the city offers. You have never left Lightfall's boundary. Your days are drills, formation work, and endless sparring against dummies carved with halos, preparing you for the fallen angels beyond the Frostmarch. The instructors talk about the north, and you listen as though it were a story you half remember. The routine is strict, and you are good at it. What you cannot shake is the feeling that you have fought something like this before.\n## Wayfarer\n### Ruin-delver\n*Wilderness - Anarchic - Academic*\n\nYou went into the old ruins on your own, hoping they would explain what you are. You scavenged for coin and clues, without license, trading relics and rumors with whoever was paying that season. You learned to read a collapsed corridor, test a floor before trusting it, and move quietly through places that have been dead since before the Age of Heroes. Automa, forgotten gods, and the floating ring all leave faint traces in the stone. Most delvers sell what they find. You keep a few pieces, just in case.\n### Laborer's Lodger\n*Urban - Communal - Labor*\n\nYou rented a bed in a laborer neighborhood for a season and stayed because nobody expected you to explain yourself. You helped where you were needed: hauling, cooking, patching roofs, minding children while their parents worked. The neighbors taught you the price of bread, whose word can be trusted, and how to share a pot among twelve. You never quite belonged, but you were never turned away, and that was worth more than you expected.\n## Familiar Trainer\n### \n### Metron Familiar Warden\n*Urban - Bureaucratic - Academic*\n\nYou asked the Mage's Guild how a person comes to keep a familiar, and they handed you a registry book and a cage of very patient ravens. The Metron licenses every bonded creature in Lightfall, so you learned the forms, the temperament tests, and the careful rituals of introduction. Wardens match small spirits to willing animals, then train both to behave in a crowded city. A familiar that misbehaves is the Measure's problem, and so is its trainer.\n", "category": "Lore", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"Playable backgrounds\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "c0fa7e90-003f-59e7-a916-f5d99ba3e574", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "backgrounds", "status": "published", "title": "Backgrounds", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "In this article you will find the baselines that the world expects frome each subclass. You need not follow them exactly. But a Undead warlock might have an idea from which source the other undead warlock draws its power.\n## Artificer\n| Subclass     | Touchpoint                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Alchemist    | The old art of turning one thing into another, handed down from sages who sought the secret of gold and the cure for every ill.   |\n| Armorer      | The forge-god's craft, which taught that bronze can be taught to stand like a hero. Armor remembers the wearers who came before.                           |\n| Artillerist  | Fire and thunder bound into a tube of metal, in the spirit of the gods' own bolts. Some say the first design fell from the sky.|\n| Battle Smith | A craft that breathes will into metal, the way golden hands once served at a divine forge. Steel follows, and sometimes it chooses to follow its own will.  |\n| Cartographer | Mapmakers are employed in service of ember watch, charting outer roads, recording where light holds and where it falls.    |\n| Reanimator   | Certain Licensed Anatomist apply the craft to heal and restore under the Metron guild and Pallor House.                    |\n## Barbarian\n| Subclass   | Touchpoint                                                                                          |\n| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Berserker  | \tA fury like the heroes of old, who fought until the field was quiet and the battle-madness left them. It rises when the rain does.       |\n| Wild Heart | The old kinship between warriors and beasts, as in the tales of lions, boars, and bulls. The wild still knows its own.       |\n| World Tree | Strength drawn from being a part of the great tree's roots, the ones most connected to the light.           |\n| Zealot     | Holy fury aimed at the cursed, whether Apollo's or an Olympian's, they are watched warily by the Temple. |\n## Bard\n| Subclass | Touchpoint                                                                                          |\n| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Dance    | The first steps taken before the first temple, when worship was movement. The body remembers what the mind forgot.  |\n| Glamour  | Charm developed by working on noble salons and Old God devotees, called a blessing by some and a snare by others. |\n| Lore     | The long thread of songs, tales, and half-remembered verses. Every story tells a little of the truth, and the bard keeps them all.                            |\n| Valor    | The courage in a marching song, and the vow that carries a lantern into the dark. It lifts those spirits of those out in weeping away from light. |\n| Moon | The silver goddess behind the clouds, still listening. Her stories are told in the dark, where the herds sleep. |\n| Spirit | The voices of heroes and the restless dead, who linger because a song is still unfinished. Summoning one is asking a favor from the past. |\n## Cleric\n| Subclass  | Touchpoint                                                                                   |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Life      | The warmth of the harvest and the hearth, healing from the Temple's almshouses and Demeter-blessed field clans.                        |\n| Light     | The god of the sun and the song, shining through whoever will carry it. A flame passed from hand to hand.          |\n| Trickery  | The laugh of the wanderer and the wine-god's mischief. Rules bend, and a clever tongue is a holy tool.   |\n| War       | The roar of the old war-gods and the discipline of the phalanx. A faith that expects its faithful to stand. |\n| Knowledge | The oracle's hunger for truth. Every answer is a candle, and every candle shows another door.                                                                                          |\n| Grave     | The ferryman's patient mercy. The dead deserve rest, and the faithful see that they get it.                                                                                   |\n| Arcana    | Priests licensed by the Metron, who treat magic as a gift to be measured and used in service of the light.                                                                                            |\n## Druid\n\n| Subclass | Touchpoint                                                                               |\n| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Land     | The soil, the grove, and the green that remembers the light which brough it to life under the weeping sky.|\n| Moon     | The moon's shapes, remembered when she is hidden. A druid takes the form of whatever the luna asks for.  |\n| Sea      | The deep water and the creatures of myth, worshipped on far eastern shores. Tide and storm obey those who learn their names. |\n| Stars    | The slow wheel of the heavens, read through gaps in the cloud. Every light overhead is an old story told in fire.  |\n## Fighter\n\n| Subclass        | Touchpoint                                                                                                                |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Arcane archer   | A shot that carries a spell with it, as the old archers were said to carry the gods' favor but now are embewed with arcane enhancements.                                                                                                                         |\n| Banneret        | Standard-bearers of the Emberwatch's road-companies and the Frostmarch garrison, trained in the art of revitalizing and rallying others with the colors of the light.                                                                                                                         |\n| Battle master   | The wisdom of old heroic strategists and the drill of the phalanx. A battle is a puzzle, and the master knows the shape of every piece.                                                        |\n| Champion        | Renown earned in the Sand Court competitions and by raw competence on the outer roads bring in the excellence, the body made perfect and a name worth singing                                                 |\n| Eldritch Knight | Steel and spellcraft learned under a Metron-licensed tutor in the light of heroes that once walked between mortal crafts and divne gifts.                                                               |\n| Psi Warrior     | Power honed within the self, this disciplined will was trained in the halls or hardened against the dread of the weeping. |\n## Monk\n\n| Subclass    | Touchpoint                                                                                                             |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Mercy       | The hand that heals and the hand that ends, both guided by the same compassion. A quiet vow to the divines to ease suffering by any means.   |\n| Shadow      | Martial artists lurking in shadowed Quarter's alley and the underground chambers of the city |\n| Mystic arts | Gymnasion-trained fighters who pair martial discipline with licensed spellcraft, a rare blend of Temple focus and Metron craft.                                                                                                                      |\n| Elements    | Deciples of westside monasteries and Metalborn forge-halls that study fire, stone, and wind.                           |\n| Open Hand   | Born of unarmed discipline from the Gymnasion's wrestlers or the Temple's silent cloisters.                            |\n## Paladin\n| Subclass     | Touchpoint                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Devotion     | Warriors of divine bronze with vows to Apollo, the Lightkeeper, and the Temple.                                                                      |\n| Glory        | Seekers of deeds worthy of a hymn, in the arena or beyond the borders of light.                                               |\n| Ancients     | Guardians of the fields and of a light an order older than the tree, Older than the rains.. their existence.. their heroics. kept quiet. |\n| Noble Genies | No genies walk Weeping, so this oath follows the four elemental spirits of earth, air, fire, and water, honored by the southern Band's caravans and the eastern volcanic islands.                                                                                                                            |\n| Vengance     | Hunters of the cursed and enforcers of the Laws of the Light against those who profit from the weeping.                      |\n## Ranger\n| Subclass      | Touchpoint                                                                                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Beast Master  | Warriors of the land with companions trained beside Emberwatch hounds or chosen from the wild edge.                   |\n| Fey Wanderer  | Warriors channeling magic from strange presences at the light's edge and minor Greek spirits of spring and grove. |\n| Gloom Stalker | Hunters in the dark beyond the towers and the drains beneath the city.                                            |\n| Hollow warden | Monstrous guardians who draw on the ancient terrors of the jagged mountains and cracked cliff's shadows ringing the realm.                                                                                                                 |\n| Hunter        | Road-scouts and monster-slayers who study the cursed and trade notes at the Bestiary Hall.                        |\n| Winter warden | Frostmarch hunters in the northern wastes, regarded with respect and fear for the cold they carry back.                                                                                                               |\n## Rogue \n| Subclass           | Touchpoint                                                                           |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Arcane Trickster   | These tricksters are tolerated by the Metron, often called Festive illusionists.     |\n| Assassin           | Quiet blades for hire in the Veil Market, or silent hands of the Order of Frostmarch.         |\n| Soulknife          | Rogues that wield their mind that glows faintly with faith or shines with pure will. |\n| Thief              | Rooftop runners and relic-lifters who slip into ruins and private collections.       |\n| Scion of the three | Agents of three dark Olympians, Ares (slaughter), Hades (death), and Kratos (tyranny), tolerated by no one and worshipped in the cellars of the Veil Market                                                                                    |\n| Phantom            | Spies and information-gatherers who walk the line between life and death, prized by the Shadowed Quarter's back-alley brokers.                                                                                    |\n## Sorcerer \n| Subclass   | Touchpoint                                                                |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Aberrant   | A whisper from the deep dark before the age of heroes, old like the Titans and strange to every hymn. It seeps through the rain and falls from the ring, and some blood answers.        |\n| Clockwork  | A quiet order of bronze and ichor, kin to the forge-god's golden hands and the giant who walked the shores. Gnomes and automa hum with the same old rhythm.      |\n| Draconic   | The memory of serpents slain by heroes, and of teeth sown into the earth that rose as warriors. Some blood still carries that old fire. |\n| Wild Magic | The laughter and discord of revels no god quite rules. It surges where it wishes and answers to no hymn, no guild, and no law. |\n| Spellfire  | A spark of the sun-god's own radiance, or of fire stolen from the heavens. The Temple calls it a gift, and the Metron calls it a danger.                                                                        |\n| Shadow     | The first night, folded into the dark beyond the tree's glow. It gathers where the lamps thin, and it knows those who walk there.                                                                         |\n## Warlock \n| Subclass      | Touchpoint                                                                   |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Archfey       | A spirit of spring, grove, or crossroads, half-remembered from the days before the rain. It laughs at the light's edge and bargains in riddles, and it is never quite what it seems..     |\n| Celestial     | A radiant presence that answers from the sun's own halls. It might be a herald of the god of light, or a fallen winged thing from beyond the north that still remembers the glow. |\n| Fiend         | A voice from the black desert's old fires or from the deep pit beneath the world, where the dead and the damned are weighed. It always offers a fair deal, and the cost comes later. |\n| Great old one | Something that watched before the gods had names. It might wait behind the ring, or hide in the storm, and it speaks in a tongue the hymns never learned.  |\n| Undead        | A mind that walked out of the grave with its memories intact. It might be a hero who refused the river of forgetting, or a patient shade of the night that tires of waiting in the dark.                                                                            |\n| Vestige       | A god whose temples have fallen to dust and whose name the rain has worn smooth. It might be a forgotten Olympian, or something far older, still hungry to be remembered.                                                                           |\n## Wizard\n\n| Subclass    | Touchpoint                                                                                       |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Abjurer     | Wards for the cleansing towers and the city's gates                                              |\n| Diviner     | Readers of the rain's pattern and portents in the ring and stars.                                |\n| Evoker      | Fire and force under Metron permit, or sharpened against fallen angels                           |\n| Illusionist | Light-weaving that borrows the tree's glow, and spectacle for holy days.                         |\n| Conjurer    | Summoners of objects and creatures, answering to the Metron's strict register.                   |\n| Enchanter   | Charm-workers of the noble salons and Rhapsodes' stages, watched for how freely they bend a will |\n| Necromancer | Metron-licensed students of death, always asked how their craft differs from the curse.          |\n| Transmuter  | Reshapers of matter in the Crafter's District and the light-wrights' benches.                    |\n", "category": "Lore", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"What could mean to be a particular adventurer\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "55df2b4a-8def-50b1-9b44-92beb30a3a27", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "classes", "status": "published", "title": "Classes", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Tasked with training personel for roadlighting. Emberwatch maintains and protects the Divine Roads of the light.\n", "category": "Faction", "frontmatter_yaml": "factionType: \"Viatory Guard\"\nalignment: \"Lawful Good\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "5dc015ed-7fd3-53b7-9f7c-b0b1bbef0125", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "emberwatch", "status": "published", "title": "Emberwatch", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Heart of light fall, protected by the Hierocracy. This artefact is was the key to nurture life under the divine tree of light. The temple protects this with their life.\n", "category": "Artifact", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"Retrieved by the first light keeper\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "61bb744c-7a4b-58c8-9802-2446192ad7f9", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "heart-of-apollo", "status": "published", "title": "Heart of Apollo", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Central ruling authority of Lightfall\n\n*Current Head Priestess: Porthila of the Light*\n\nThe temple as its head of state, the city of lightfall flourishes under the guidance from the Oracle and priests of Apollo. The Hierocracy supersedes the Nobles council, Mage's guild and the Emberwatch. Standing under the authority of the Lightkeeper and along the Inquisition.\n", "category": "Faction", "frontmatter_yaml": "factionType: \"Ecclesiocracy\"\nalignment: \"Lawful Neutral\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "c3d4ca10-a3fb-5f9b-8c07-e5c235c1e9db", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "hierocracy-of-lightfall", "status": "published", "title": "Hierocracy of Lightfall", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Established by the second lightkeeper 'Aetós II' , Descendents of the 7 Apostles have earned titles by completing a divine quest to the north, granting them nobility. Working under the Hierocracy these nobles are tasked to oversee major administrative functions of the city.\n", "category": "Faction", "frontmatter_yaml": "factionType: \"Political / Governing / Aristocratic\"\nalignment: \"Lawful Neutral\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "42a7785c-30d4-5827-8621-98e0c4b0d6d4", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "high-council", "status": "published", "title": "High Council", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "*The Prayer-Bearer, First Lightkeeper of the weeping realm*\n## The One Who Knelt\nWhen the cursed rain had taken nearly everything, Katsík was still walking. They were not a king, a warrior, or a priest, only one of the weary, kneeling in the mud when the last hope had thinned to almost nothing. They prayed, and Apollo answered. The god poured himself into a seed of light, and from that seed grew the great tree. Katsík was the first soul to stand beneath its branches and the first to be chosen by its light.\n## Founder of the City of Light\nKatsík the first Lightkeeper did not build Lightfall with stone or law. Those came later, under other future Lightkeepers. Katsík's work turned a crowd of survivors into a society.\n\nThe people who gathered under the tree were exhausted, grieving, and afraid, and many of them had lost someone to the rain twice: once in death, and again when the dead returned. Katsík taught them that the tree was not a camp to be endured but a home to be kept. The first dwellings rose around the roots, and the first vigil-fires were lit in the tree's own light. The place was named Lightfall, for where the light fell.\n## People under the light\nThe journey to north had already broken down most of the old divides. Katsík finished the work. Every species had prayed together for the seed, and Katsík held that anyone who had prayed for the tree had a place beneath it. No people were set above another, and no old quarrel was allowed to follow anyone through the gate.\nFrom this came the city's oldest custom: those who shelter beneath the tree are one people. The mixed, many-tongued Lightfall of today grew from that promise.\n## Quest for the Heart of Apollo\nA city cannot live on prayer alone. Katsík and 7 apostles on a divine quest far south returned with the divine relic now known as 'Heart of Apollo'. The divine relic cleansing the cursed rains and returning life to the realm with pure water. The holy tree providing ample of both warmth and water, led to the first harvest in the city of light.\n## Legacy\nKatsík is remembered as the Lightkeeper who prayed, and also as the one who stayed. They began the city's unity and its farmland and left both to those who followed, and the Agora still stands on what they founded.\n", "category": "NPC", "frontmatter_yaml": "role: \"First Lightkeeper\"\naffiliation: \"Hierocracy of Lightfall\"\nstatus: \"dead\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "78ef497d-2a81-5b80-a835-766d4f39e1e9", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "katsik-i", "status": "published", "title": "Katsík I", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "## Civil rights  \n- All sentient humaniods untouched by the weeping are given the\n-  Right to Live\n    - Enjoy freedom of property and speech\n    - Right to a fair trial\n    - To bear arms in unbounded areas and protect themselves and their loved ones and their property\n  \n## Civil Duties  \n- Every citizen is barred from giving themselves in to the weeping.\n- Every citizen that owns property within the light shall pay taxes to the Lightkeeper.\n- Every citizen must uphold the law.\n- Every citizen must help in the defense of the light and the lightkeeper.\n- Every nobleman must collect taxes in the name of the light for defense and prosperity of the lands they are entrusted to administrate.\n## Criminal law:  \n  \nYou shall not murder  \n- This crime shall be punished by Heavy fine, Temple service or even Road Lighting.\n- If the murderer is identified but is not caught, their immediate relatives or guild members shall pay heavy fines or forced into Temple service.\n  \nYou shall not steal  \n- Crime shall be punished by heavy fine or Temple service.\n  \nYou shall not put the light or the Lighbearer in danger both by action or inaction.  \n- Doing so shall be punished by Road lighting or Death.\n\n## Magic law:  \n  \nAll mages, magical conduits, magic practitioners or individual with magical potency shall be registered under the Mage guild and shall have their magical amulet visible during all spell casting.  \n- Failure in doing so shall be punished with fines or temple service.\n\nAll mages shall co-operate with the Inquisition\n\n### Magic law amendment 1:  \nPractice of Necromancy* is restricted by the law and can only be done with the permission of the Deceased.  \n- Malpractice shall be punished by fines, Confiscation of magical focus, tomes and Temple service.\n  \nNo one shall summon demons, fiends and other uncontrollable creatures that may cause damage or harm to innocents.  \n- Doing so may be punished by fines, Temple service or even Road lighting.\n\n### Magic law amendment 2:    \nNo one shall cast a spell on others without the permit of the target or by someone appointed by the Lightkeeper.  \n- The only exception to this rule are healing and resurrection spells, but all healers should be registered and wear visibly their healer badge or amulet.\n\nNo one shall disguise as or deceive others pretending to be a registered mage, healer or appointed official.  \n-  Doing so is punished with Temple service, Road lighting or even exile.\n", "category": "Lore", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"Laws of Lightfall\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "2f49078f-8323-5711-a4b1-8475ecdc4f75", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "laws-of-the-light", "status": "published", "title": "Laws of the Light", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "*The Lion-Marcher, Fourth Lightkeeper of Lightfall*\n\nAfter Aetós gave Lightfall its walls and Távros gave it a way of living, Apollo's light chose Leander. Where the earlier Lightkeepers built inward, Leander looked outward. Under the fourth Lightkeeper, Lightfall stopped being a refuge to hold and became a realm to extend, and the city's borders reach farther than they ever have.\n## Expansion of Territory\nLeander's reign is defined by the push beyond the boundary towers.\n- The North: Raiders had long harried the northern edge of the realm. Leander drove them back and conquered the north, and gave divine light to the Snow Eye castle. Which now protects the realm from the North.\n- The South: Leander secured the southern timber, giving a city of stone and fields the wood it needed to keep growing. In doing so Leander also encountered the Anaptirs.\n## Contact with the Anaptir\nOn the southern road, Leander's people met the Tribe of Anaptir, known by the magical bands worn on their right hands. Contact became trade, and trade became the Band's Embassy, where their caravans arrive every two to three months.\n## The Three Foundations\n**The Emberwatch.** Leander founded the Emberwatch to keep the roads lit. Its companies raise lanterns and posts along the outer roads, carrying divine light into the weeping so that travelers have a trail of warmth to follow. Its Watcher, Sailas, sits on the council.\n\n**The Order of Frostmarch.** Leander founded the Order of Frostmarch to guard the northern borders. Its Quarters train defenders against the threats beyond the conquered north, and the fallen angels are chief among them.\n\n**The Order of Sol Invictus**. Leander founded the Order of Sol Invictus to spread light across the land. Its Solarii are the light-blessed, and its Archons sit on the council. The order is the Lightkeeper's reach made into people.\n## The Eastern March\nLeander is currently marching east to reclaim the light on the shores. The campaign is ongoing, and the city waits on news from the road.\n", "category": "NPC", "frontmatter_yaml": "role: \"Fourth Lightkeeper\"\naffiliation: \"Hierocracy of Lightfall\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "604079dd-ffba-5251-9238-dfd5f5a459a3", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "leander-iv", "status": "published", "title": "Leander IV", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "| Type | City and realm |\n|---|---|\n| Ruler |\tLeander IV, Fourth Lightkeeper (on campaign in the East) |\n| Founded by | Katsík I, First Lightkeeper |\n| Patron deity | Apollo |\n| Age | Over a Century |\n| Population | Mixed humanoids of every species |\n| Government | Hierocracy with a Lightkeeper head and a six-seat council | \n|Currency\t| Gold, silver, and copper stamped with the great tree, though most folk barter|\n\nLightfall is the only known shelter in a world drowned by the weeping, a cursed, magical rain that has fallen for over a century. The city stands around a vast divine tree of light, a gift of Apollo. Beneath its branches the curse cannot reach, and a civilization has grown up there. Fields green with harvest surround it, held by a people who were never meant to survive and thrive anyway.\n## Geography and Layout\n\n![Lightfall Map](https://i.ibb.co/pcRJqJf/Weeping.webp)\n\n\nLightfall is a round city at the foot of the great tree, enclosed by a grey stone wall and a pale moat with a single bridge at the southern gate. Beyond the wall lie fields in green, gold, and ochre, threaded by streams and watched by small towers. Jagged mountains and cracked cliffs ring the whole realm.\n### Notable Locations\n- Agora of the Lightkeeper: The tallest structure in the city and seat of the Lightkeeper. It stands empty while Leander is away.\n- Inquisition of the Light: Founded under Távros to enforce the Laws of the Light. It also controls the punching, issuing, and authentication of coin.\n- Granary of Aetós: Built in an era of plenty, and still the symbol of the city's abundance.\n- Temple District: Dedicated to Apollo and the other gods. Citizens gather here at dawn and dusk to pray.\n- Sol Invictus: Home to the Solarii, who represent Lightfall's hope.\n- Crafter's District: Smiths, armorers, light-wrights, outfitters, herbalists, alchemists, and more, around a plaza with a slender obelisk.\n- Emberwatch's Barracks: Home of the company that keeps the outer roads lit.\n- Frostmarch's Quarters: The training ground for defenders against the fallen angels beyond the Frostmarch.\n- Band's Embassy: Marks the southern road and its trade route\n\nBeyond the wall are the farmlands, the cleansing watchtowers along the boundary, and the outer roads.\n## Government and Society\nLightfall is a hierocracy: a realm ruled through the church of Apollo. Authority flows from the god's light, to the Lightkeeper he chooses, and down through the Temple and the institutions that serve it. Crown and altar are one structure here, and there is no separate secular power for the Lightkeeper to answer to.\n### The Divine Order\n\nApollo is the source of all legitimate authority. His light chooses each Lightkeeper, not by blood or vote, and the whole realm treats that choice as final.\n\nThe Lightkeeper stands at the top as both sovereign and high holy figure. Their word is law and doctrine alike, and their authority covers Lightfall in full: faith, war, law, and harvest. Under Leander IV, that authority is currently carried on campaign in the East.\n\nThe Temple, led by Headpriest Porthila of the Light, is the church of Apollo and the body through which the god's will is interpreted and enacted. It keeps the dawn and dusk prayers, tends the flame, and, in the Lightkeeper's absence, stands as the heart of the realm's faith and the first voice of its government.\n### The Council of the Light\nBeneath the Lightkeeper, six seats serve directly as their council. Power of each seat is varyied and changing, the only agreement being the high seat of the council Rests with the Temple. \n### How Authority Works\n- Law is sacred. The Laws of the Light are religious law, and the Inquisition enforces them as such. Breaking the law is a failing before the god as well as the city.\n- Coin is blessed. Coins bear the great tree, and the Inquisition holds sole authority to punch, issue, and authenticate them.\n- Magic is licensed. The Metron answers to the Lightkeeper and regulates magic so that it never rivals the divine light.\n- Land is held in trust. The Noble Houses, descended from the apostles of the first holy quest, hold land and administer districts, taxation, and trades beneath the Lightkeeper. Their standing comes from service to the light.\n- The guilds serve the order. Crafters, healers, and other trades are organized into guilds, each overseen by the institutions above them.\n- The armed orders carry the light outward. The citywatch, The Apostles, The Emberwatch, the Order of Frostmarch, and the Order of Sol Invictus are the Lightkeeper's reach beyond the walls.\n## Society\n\n*Lightfall is in an early feudal stage. Lords and houses manage taxation and the economic and social life of the city, but they do so as stewards of a sacred order.*\n\n- Faith binds the people. Most citizens follow Apollo and the religion of Light. Devotion to the Lightkeeper, and in their absence the Temple, is deep, and faith holds people together through hardship.\n- Species stand equal beneath the tree. Katsík held that anyone who prayed for the tree has a place beneath it, and the old divides were set aside.\n- Other faiths are tolerated, not equal. The Old Gods of the Greek pantheon may be worshipped, but Apollo is expected to stay on top. Devotees who forget it find the Temple's silence and their neighbors' cooler welcome.\n- Dissent is quiet. Small cults hold that the weeping is retribution against humanity. They have no place in the sacred order and keep out of sight.\n## Economy\n\nLightfall is still mostly agrarian. Surplus harvests under Aetós allowed specialized trades to appear. Crafters flourished under Távros, and defenders and territorial growth came under Leander. Coins are stamped with the great tree, but the city is close-knit and mostly runs on barter.\n\nThe southern Anaptir trade through the Band's Embassy, arriving by caravan every two to three months. Leander's campaigns also secured timber from the south.\n\n## Life in the City\nDaily rhythm: Citizens gather in the Temple District each dawn and dusk to pray.\nThe rain's pattern: The divine forecast shapes harvests, travel, and expeditions.\nLeaving the light: A chosen few go on divine quests beyond the realm. Some people train to resist the weeping, but it still takes its toll, and no one can say who will hold. Every trip out is a risk.\nTrades and posts: Lightfall's people work as healers, weather-seers, crafters, light-wrights, farmers, channel-tenders, lamplighters, guards, scribes, rhapsodes, caravan hands, and delvers into the old ruins, among many other callings.\n## Outskirts\n\n![Hexflower - Lightfall](https://i.ibb.co/q3W80pYX/Tree-of-light.png)\n", "category": "Lore", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"The city of Apollo\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "e291c9d3-5294-5099-b34a-47370e9bb9d9", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "lightfall", "status": "published", "title": "Lightfall", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "This prism of the sun god is prophesied to unite the realm when held by the light keeper.\n", "category": "Artifact", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"Promised savior\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "cd5f89cc-df5b-5e80-8acc-db5e38189fab", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "prism-of-light", "status": "published", "title": "Prism of Light", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "[Common name (Specific name)]\n## Humans (Anthropoi)\nHumans are the most common species in Weeping, and their ability to train and develop resistance to the cursed rains has earned them key positions in the Hierocracy. Legend says that humans from across the realms travelled to Weeping during the Age of Heroes.\n\n*Today in Lightfall: Humans of all ehnicity roam lightfall, they fill most seats of the Temple, the Inquisition, and the council beneath the empty Agora.*\n## Dwarves (Daktyloi)\nLiving longer than humans, these sturdy folk spend most of their lives devoted to metal or stone craft. Many feel a natural calling to create one masterpiece from the earth itself.\n\n*Today in Lightfall: You'll find them in the Crafter's District, working stone and iron for the city they helped build.*\n## Metalborn Dwarves (Khalkeoi)\nAfter generations spent mastering the forge, this subspecies of dwarves was blessed by Hephaestus, god of the forge. They embody the forge itself, and their metallic bodies serve as their own shields.\n\n*Today in Lightfall: Their gleaming frames are a common sight at the forges and among the Emberwatch's ranks.*\n## Orcs (Skythai)\nMost orcs trace their origins to the desert of the far east, where they lived as nomads. Many mark themselves with tattoos and body paints that show their social standing and achievements.\n\n*Today in Lightfall: Their painted marks are easy to spot, helping and carrying the city's markets, where the old nomad habit of travelling light persists.*\n## Gnomes (Daidalidai)\nGnomes are a curious people forever chasing their own beginnings. Their oldest records say they were found at the end of a mechanical labyrinth, and every gnome since has been a little bit of a maze-walker, collecting clues, keeping threads, and cheerfully refusing to stop asking.\n\n*Today In Lightfall: Citizens smile when a gnome stops mid-stride to study the paving stones or a dead-end alley, then politely wait to see whether they find what they were looking for.*\n## Halflings (Horaioi)\nHalflings were the first farmers to emerge after the weeping began. These peace-loving folk love to nurture and till the land through the divine channels, and they rarely leave the farmlands. They are also very particular about time and punctuality.\n\n*Today in Lightfall: They tend the fields beyond the wall, and it's said the Granary's deliveries arrive on the hour, to the minute.*\n## Tieflings (Kerastai)\nDescendants of the black desert, these half-demons are known for using steam and magic to power through their foes and daily life. The hotblooded theifling sociert measures status by how richly their horns and tails are decorated.\n\n*Today in Lightfall: Their steam-driven craft is welcome in the workshops, though the Metron watches their magic closely.*\n## Aasimar (Ouranidai)\nThe aasimar descend from an unknown angel that once came down to the northern wastelands. These divine-blooded people live in tribal societies and yearn to understand and follow divine will.\n\n*Today in Lightfall: Many find a calling in the Temple and in Sol Invictus, serving a light they feel they were born to understand.*\n## Dragonborn (Spartoi)\nThe dragonborn hail from the eastern volcanic islands, where tradition is bound to honor, blade, and breath. They were once revered for being an elite hunting force slaying monsters the size of mountains.\n\n*In Lightfall today: Many serve in the Emberwatch and at the Frostmarch's Quarters, where monster-slaying still earns the highest honor.*\n## Goliath (Gigantes)\nThese half-giants mark their clans with different body paints. Each clan holds the others responsible for a grudge so ancient that no one remembers its cause.\n\n*In Lightfall today: Their clan paints are a common sight at the Frostmarch's Quarters, where their strength is turned against the fallen angels beyond.*\n## Cyclops\nConsidered descendants of evil, the cyclopes were once subjugated during the Age of Heroes and forced to fight against evil. Only after the weeping began were they welcomed back into society.\n\n*In Lightfall today: They walk openly beneath the tree, though some elders still whisper about the banished Ruler of the Skies.*\n\n## Automa (Warforged)\nThe automa are creations of divine will. Their bronze-and-ceramic bodies are often linked to many different gods, but scholars have yet to reach a consensus on their origins. Most are found in ruins older than the Age of Heroes.\n\n*In Lightfall today: Automa recovered from ruins during divine quests are studied, and welcomed, in the city.*\n\n## Aphotics (Dhampir)\nThe lightless ones were once hunted as blood drinkers. When the clouds of the weeping covered the sun, they became the greatest allies of humankind. After aiding the Lightkeeper on divine quests, they were finally accepted in parts of the city, and today they walk the shadowy alleyways, tolerated by the Inquisition.\n\n*In Lightfall today: They keep to the alleys beyond the tree's brightest glow, and most citizens still cross the street to avoid them.*\n\n## Lykaonoi (Lupin)\n\nOnce believed lost to time, these warrior canines were found beneath the weeping sky in a surprisingly human form. Some succumb to a rabid urge on the brightest nights, so the species is closely monitored by the Metron.\n\n*In Lightfall today: The Metron keeps a register of every Lykaonoi, and on clear nights they are often asked to stay indoors.*\n\n## Baskanoi (Hexblood)\nThe baskanoi wear crowns of wood that glow with an eerie light. Once lost to wild magic, they were brought back under control by the light of Apollo and given a second chance in civilization because of their resistance to the weeping.\n\n*In Lightfall today: Their glowing crowns are seen as a sign of Apollo's mercy, and many serve near the Temple District.*\n", "category": "Lore", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"Playable species desc.\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "d2f26ce6-946d-5d69-a036-576f4da05520", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "species-in-weeping", "status": "published", "title": "Species in Weeping", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "*Visionary of the great city of Lightfall*\n## The Third Light\nAfter Aetós, Apollo's light chose Távros, and Lightfall turned from filling its stores to improving how its people lived. Távros inherited a population that no longer feared hunger. Where earlier Lightkeepers had asked how the people would survive, Távros asked how they would live well. Under the third Lightkeeper, Lightfall grew from a fortified refuge into a true city.\n## Builder of the Lightfall\nMost of what makes Lightfall livable today was made in Távros's reign. Roads were laid, districts were drawn, and water was brought in and carried away again, so the people enjoy a clean and healthy life that few in the weeping could dream of. Sickness that once spread freely through crowded streets grew rare. Homes, markets, and workshops were set in orderly places, and the city's management took the shape it still keeps.\n## Guilds of the Crafters and the Mages\nThe surplus of Aetós had freed hands for trade. Távros gave those trades structure. Smiths, armorers, light-wrights, herbalists, alchemists, and other craftspeople were organized into guilds and gathered into the Crafter's District, where standards were set and skills passed on.\n\nTávros also founded the Metron, the Mage's Guild, to measure and regulate the use of magic in Lightfall. In a world where a curse is made of magic, a Lightkeeper who wished the city to be safe could not leave its use unwatched. The Metron licensed those who practiced, and the Measure has kept watch ever since.\n## The Boundaries and the Cleansing Towers\nTávros's most lasting work lies beyond the walls. Távros marked the boundaries of the city and raised cleansing watchtowers along them. The towers hold the cursed at bay and give shelter to the brave souls who venture into the weeping, so a traveler returning from the dark has somewhere to run. The small towers that watch over the fields today descend from that work.\n## The Inquisition\nThe Inquisition of the Light was also founded in Távros's reign, to enforce and uphold the Laws of the Light. A city that grows this large needs law as well as walls, and the Inquisition became that law. It also holds the right to punch, issue, and authenticate the city's coin.\n## The Legacy\nKatsík gave Lightfall its people, and Aetós gave it walls and stores. Távros gave it a way of living. The clean streets, the guilds, the boundary towers, and the ordered districts are all his work. The city today is largely the one he built, and the fourth Lightkeeper, Leander, would take it beyond its borders.\n", "category": "NPC", "frontmatter_yaml": "role: \"Third Lightkeeper\"\naffiliation: \"Hierocracy of Lightfall\"\nstatus: \"dead\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "4881a864-a813-5cc9-b590-a902f64f382e", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "tavros-iii", "status": "published", "title": "Távros III", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "The third divine quest ended with the leader of fallen angels, his crimson halo is the symbol of apostle's victory over the north.\n", "category": "Artifact", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"Symbol of victory over the fallen angels of the north\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "515e7633-bf65-5c03-8fb2-0fb83c393f65", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "the-crimson-halo", "status": "published", "title": "The Crimson Halo", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Mage's guild, the measure, was created to regulate use of arcane magic in the city of Lightfall. It currently works as a training and research institution for the city. Many of its mages are tasked to maintain quality of life in and around lightfall.\n", "category": "Faction", "frontmatter_yaml": "factionType: \"Mage's guild\"\nalignment: \"Neutral\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "af26b6d2-b381-5134-a0f4-a10097d3b52f", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "the-metron", "status": "published", "title": "The Metron", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "## The Rain That Does Not Stop\nFor over a century, the rain has fallen. It is not water as the old world knew it. It is a cursed, magical downpour that does more than wear life away: it corrupts it. What the weeping touches does not simply die. It changes, and it turns against everything that still lives.\n\nOut in the drenched dark, monsters wait. Once they were beasts and people like any other, and now they hunger for the living. They drag the living into the rain and remake them in their own image, human and beast alike. The world beyond the light is not empty. It is full, and it is watching.\n## The point of Light\n\nIn this part of the world, there is one refuge: the divine tree of light, raised by the will of Apollo. Beneath its branches the curse cannot reach, and beneath them a civilization has lived for more than a hundred years. That city is Lightfall. Generations have been born, grown old, and died under the tree without ever feeling the rain on their skin.\n## Outriders\nNot everyone stays beneath the branches. A chosen few venture out on the divine quests beyond the realm, though their numbers are small. Some have also been trained by the temple to resist the weeping. Training helps, but it does not save anyone. The rain still takes its toll, and no one can say who will hold and who will break, or when. Every step into the weeping is a gamble.\n## The Pattern of the Rain\nThe divine will grants a mercy: the weather can be foretold. The strength of the rain rises and falls in a pattern that repeats year after year, and the people of Lightfall have learned to live by it. They know the rhythms and plan their lives around the storms, as sailors do the tides.\n## The Clear Days\nThen there are the rare days when the clouds thin and scatter. On those days, the people beneath the light look up and see what the rain usually hides: a faint, sparse line, a ring floating through the heavens, drifting slowly around the plane. No one in Lightfall can say what it is, only that it is there.\n\nA world full of mystery, and of weeping, awaits.\n", "category": "Lore", "frontmatter_yaml": "summary: \"What is weeping?\"\n", "kind": "codex_entry", "public_id": "8e2e307d-2888-5e3f-b278-ae03e400df49", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "weeping", "status": "published", "title": "Weeping", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
