               | Very Rare | 5     | 2     | 5    |\n| Red Dragon Scale Mail                             | Very Rare | 2     | 8     | 2    |\n| Regal Breastplate of the Tyrant (*)               | Very Rare | 1     | 8     | 3    |\n| Resistant Blade of the Guardian                   | Very Rare | 5     | 5     | 2    |\n| Ring of Regeneration                              | Very Rare | 0     | 8     | 4    |\n| Ring of Shooting Stars                            | Very Rare | 3     | 0     | 9    |\n| Ring of Telekinesis                               | Very Rare | 0     | 1     | 11   |\n| Robe of Scintillating Colors                      | Very Rare | 1     | 2     | 9    |\n| Robe of Stars                                     | Very Rare | 3     | 2     | 7    |\n| Rod of Absorption                                 | Very Rare | 0     | 5     | 7    |\n| Rod of Alertness                                  | Very Rare | 2     | 6     | 4    |\n| Rod of Security                                   | Very Rare | 0     | 6     | 6    |\n| Scimitar of Speed                                 | Very Rare | 8     | 1     | 3    |\n| Shield of the Cavalier                            | Very Rare | 2     | 8     | 2    |\n| Silver Dragon Scale Mail                          | Very Rare | 2     | 8     | 2    |\n| Somniferous Blossom Rod                           | Very Rare | 0     | 1     | 11   |\n| Spell Scroll (Level 6)                            | Very Rare | 0     | 1     | 11   |\n| Spell Scroll (Level 7)                            | Very Rare | 0     | 1     | 11   |\n| Spell Scroll (Level 8)                            | Very Rare | 0     | 2     | 10   |\n| Spellguard Shield                                 | Very Rare | 1     | 7     | 4    |\n| Spirit Board                                      | Very Rare | 0     | 2     | 10   |\n| Staff of Fire                                     | Very Rare | 5     | 0     | 7    |\n| Staff of Frost                                    | Very Rare | 4     | 2     | 6    |\n| Staff of Power                                    | Very Rare | 4     | 2     | 6    |\n| Staff of Striking                                 | Very Rare | 6     | 0     | 6    |\n| Staff of Thunder and Lightning                    | Very Rare | 5     | 0     | 7    |\n| Sword of Sharpness                                | Very Rare | 9     | 0     | 3    |\n| Thunderous Greatclub                              | Very Rare | 9     | 1     | 2    |\n| Tome of Clear Thought                             | Very Rare | 0     | 2     | 10   |\n| Tome of Leadership and Influence                  | Very Rare | 0     | 3     | 9    |\n| Tome of Understanding                             | Very Rare | 0     | 2     | 10   |\n| Tramontane Armor                                  | Very Rare | 1     | 9     | 2    |\n| Wand of Polymorph                                 | Very Rare | 2     | 2     | 8    |\n| White Dragon Scale Mail                           | Very Rare | 2     | 8     | 2    |\n| Workshop Wrecker                                  | Very Rare | 4     | 0     | 8    |\n", "category": "Systems", "frontmatter_yaml": "emoji: \"🪄\"\nwip: \"false\"\nsummary: \"Rules about how many magic items you can carry and how to make them.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "8357dc6f-b41d-5344-a6ea-18343cd6d81c", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "magic-items", "status": "published", "title": "Magic Items", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "In LiCh, a Mini-Campaign is defined as a series of quests with the same Characters and Dungeon Master that runs anywhere between a minimum of 2 sessions and a maximum of 12 sessions.\n## Game-play Modes\nBasically, a Mini-Campaign is just a new gameplay mode. So, the two gameplay modes existing in the server become - One Shots and Mini-Campaign.\n\nThose familiar with video games will already be aware of gameplay modes, but basically it just means that the core game and rules remain the same. It is essentially a few small changes to create a new perspective.\n## How Mini-Campaigns differ from One Shots?\n- Singular DM across multiple sessions.\n- Characters are locked into the Mini-Campaign until it resolves or is abandoned.\n- Players can make new characters for the Mini-Campaign which do not take character slots. However, these characters retire at the end of Mini-Campaign unless the player wishes to claim them with one of their slots.\n\nA Mini-Campaign is considered **Resolved** when the DM thinks the player goal for it has been completed. Similarly, it is considered to be **Abandoned** when the DM declares it to be so.\n\nIn LiCh a character normally is available to the player 24/7, this instead changes to the character only being available to the player during the sessions of the Mini-Campaign, like it would be in a normal campaign.\n\nSo, no downtime, no access to any Text RP outside the Mini-Campaign, no out of session buffs, rests, interacting with other characters etc. This what it means for a character to be locked in.\n## What about everything else?\nXP? Magic items? Locations? Quest posts? Summaries?  \nYou follow server rules. Literally for anything other than what I have mentioned in the section above, follow server rules and etiquette.\n\nYou make a single quest post for a Mini-Campaign, and you schedule all sessions in the thread of that quest post. The players must write a summary for each game in the Mini-Campaign.\n\nAs for short and long rests, you take them within in the Mini-Campaign whenever your DM allows you to take them.\n", "category": "Systems", "frontmatter_yaml": "emoji: \"🐉\"\nwip: \"false\"\nsummary: \"Rules for running Mini-Campaigns.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "5f47a6d8-819e-5faa-8554-8ea30336966c", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "mc", "status": "published", "title": "Mini-Campaigns", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Hello and Welcome to Living Chronicles. I am Ashwin or Mortal on discord, the community manager of this little corner of escapism. \n\nWe play D&D here, lots and lots of D&D. If you are not familiar with what that is - [Check it out here.](/rules/guides/welcome-to-d-d)\n\nLiving Chronicles is a Community Campaign. You share a singular world with a hundreds of other players and dozens of DMs who run games in it and progress the story together.\n\nNow before we go any further - If you are a person that prefers talking rather than reading, please go to [this channel](https://discord.com/channels/1278898468970299523/1278900614641750150) and tag @onboarding and ask for help they will guide you through everything, if you don't mind reading and learning at your own pace, soldier on.\n\nThis is mostly a community that plays online so the minimum requirements you need are - A non-archaic computing device with browsing capabilities, a steady internet connection, and a serviceable mic. If you already use google meet or zoom, you are good to go 👍\n\nNow you need to make a character, Don't worry just follow the steps [here](/rules/guides/character-creation)\n\nDone? Take a moment to read through the [gameplay guide](/rules/guides/gameplay-guide), it has essential rules and expectations.\n\nAnd that's it, you are good to play ❤️Head on over to the Quest Board and signup to games you find interesting. \n## But there is more!\nThere is so much more for you to explore - Check out the lore of Weeping in the codex, go through the downtime system to see what more you can do, check out the bots guide to know how avrae works, start roleplaying over text with other players in channels... and the list goes on. There is lots more for you here as all our community veterans can tell you. Take your time, look around and ask any questions you have in the [game support](https://discord.com/channels/1278898468970299523/1278900614641750150) channel. \n\nBe Curious, See ya soon.\n", "category": "Getting started", "frontmatter_yaml": "emoji: \"🆕\"\nwip: \"false\"\nsummary: \"A guide for those who are new to this community.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "a71213a3-3bbe-5953-abb2-d5d66e45fb12", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "new-player-guide", "status": "published", "title": "New Player Guide", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "## PCXP & Tiers\n- After level 10, all XP goes into a **Post-Cap XP (PCXP)** Pool.\n- PCXP is spent on **Advancements,** Special Feats instead of new level ups.\n- Spend PCXP to gain new advancements.\n- You may take any Advancement, so long as you meet the prerequisite of the tier and the advancement. The available advancements are detailed below.\n- Like all other feats, advancement feats can only be taken once unless the feat specifies otherwise.\n\n| Tier | PCXP Cost |\n| ---- | --------- |\n| 1    | 5000      |\n| 2    | 10000     |\n| 3    | 20000     |\n| 4    | 40000     |\n\n## Tier 1\n### Origin Feat\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - None\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None \n- **Feat Description** - You may choose one origin feat of your choice and take it. You may take this feat multiple times, choosing a different option each time.\n### Ethereal Sight\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - None\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None \n- **Feat Description** - Once per short rest as a bonus action, you can open your third eye for 10 minutes. During this period, you have the benefits of the spell See Invisibility.\n### Silver Step\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - None\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None \n- **Feat Description** - Once per short rest, as a Bonus Action you can move up to twice your speed without provoking opportunity attacks.\n### Flame Strike\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - None\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None\n- **Feat Description** - Once per short rest, when you hit with a weapon/unarmed attack you may channel a spark of primordial flame to deal an additional 2d8 fire damage.\n## Tier 2\n### Death Throes\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 1 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None \n- **Feat Description** - Once per long rest when your character would fall unconscious, you can instead choose to take an action, you cannot heal yourself in this action. Immediately after your action is resolved, you fall unconscious.\n### Additional Spell - 2\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 1 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Spellcasting or Pact Magic Feature, 2nd level+ spell slots \n- **Feat Description** - You may choose one level 2 spell from your class list and learn that spell. This spell is always prepared for you. You can cast this spell once per long rest without using a spell slot. You may take this feat multiple times, choosing a different option each time.\n### Hunter's Fury\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 1 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Ranger Class, No Multi-classing \n- **Feat Description** - Once per short rest when you cast hunter’s mark, you can choose for it to not take concentration.\n### Explosive Strike\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 1 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None\n- **Feat Description** - Once per short rest, when you hit with a weapon/unarmed attack you may channel a spark of primordial earth and flame to deal an additional 3d8 bludgeoning damage and force the target to make STR save (DC = 8+Prof+Str/Dex) on a failure the target is pushed 30 ft horizontally in any direction of your choice.\n### Wild Magic Circle\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 1 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Spellcasting or Pact Magic Feature, Additional Spell - 2 advancement. \n- **Feat Description** - \n- Once per long rest, using a bonus action you can create a 10 ft radius circle of wild magic on the ground, the circle lasts for 1 minute. \n- While you stand within the bounds of this circle for any spell that you cast of 3rd level or higher roll on the Wild Magic Table from the wild magic sorcerer. \n- If your character is already a Wild Magic sorcerer with more than 6 levels in that class, you may roll twice and choose the result you like for each instance that you trigger wild magic within the circle.\n### Squad, Move Up!\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 1 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None \n- **Feat Description** - Once per long rest as bonus action, you can call out to the entire party, all heroes can use their reaction to move up to their speed.\n## Tier 3\n### General Feat\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 2 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None \n- **Feat Description** - You may choose one general feat of your choice and take it.\n### Additional Spell - 3\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 2 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Spellcasting or Pact Magic Feature, 3rd level+ spell slots \n- **Feat Description** - You may choose two level 3 spells from your class list and learn those spells. These spells are always prepared for you. You may take this feat multiple times, choosing different options each time.\n### Slaying Strike\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 2 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None\n- **Feat Description** - Once per long rest, when you hit with a weapon/unarmed attack you may channel a spark of primordial earth and water to deal an additional 4d8 acid damage and force the target to make CON save (DC = 8+Prof+Str/Dex) On a failure the target is vulnerable to the next attack that hits. On a success the target can’t heal until the start of its next turn or villain action.\n### Hunter’s Focus\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 2 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Ranger Class, No Multi-classing\n- **Feat Description** - Hunter’s Mark no longer takes concentration for you. Once per long rest you can spend an additional level 3 spell slot when casting Hunter’s mark, if you do so the additional damage from the spell increases to 1d8 for the next minute.\n## Tier 4\n### Third Attack\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 3 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Extra Attack Feature, No Multiclassing\n- **Feat Description** - You can make an additional attack as part of the attack action.\n### Resistance\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 3 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None\n- **Feat Description** - You are resistant to one damage type of your choosing.\n### Fortitude\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 3 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - None\n- **Feat Description** - You have advantage on a saving throw of your choosing.\n### Additional Spell - 5\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 3 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Spellcasting or Pact Magic Feature. 5th+ spell slots.\n- **Feat Description** - You may choose two level 5 spells from your class list and learn those spells. These spells are always prepared for you. You must be able to cast level 3 spells in the class to learn spells from that class’s spell list.\n### Primordial Circle\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 3 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Spellcasting or Pact Magic Feature\n- **Feat Description** - Once per long rest, using a bonus action you can create a 15 ft radius circle of primordial elements on the ground, the circle lasts for 1 minute.\n- While you stand within the bounds of this circle any spell that you cast that deals elemental damage, deals an additional 2d8 damage of the same kind.\n- While you stand within the bounds of this circle, any elemental damage you take is resisted.\n### Hesitation is Weakness\n- **Tier Prerequisite** - At least one Tier 3 advancement\n- **Advancement Prerequisite** - Rogue Class, No Multiclassing\n- **Feat Description** - Once per long rest, before an enemy starts their turn in combat, you can immediately take a full turn. This is in addition to your turn as noted in the initiative order.\n", "category": "Systems", "frontmatter_yaml": "emoji: \"📈\"\nwip: \"false\"\nsummary: \"Rules for progression past level 10.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "39e83d84-e30b-5d44-bba5-fa4b23a2d73b", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "pcxp", "status": "published", "title": "Post-Cap Progression", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "**Prerequisite** - 6 or more levels in Martial Classes i.e. classes that are not half casters or full casters.\n\nSo, the classes that you need are Fighter, Barbarian, Rogue or Monk.\n## Stamina\nYou have maximum **Stamina** equal to your Strength/Dexterity Modifier (Your choice). All stamina is regained when you take a Short or Long Rest.\n## Leveling Up\nWhen you reach Martial Level 10 your Stamina Maximum increases by 2.\n### Definitions\nThe following are some terms used in the table below.\n- **Free Reaction** - Follows the same rules as normal reactions but these reactions can be taken any number of time per round so long as its trigger is met.\n- **Character DC** - 8 + Proficiency Bonus + Highest Stat Modifier except for Constitution.\n- **Shift -** When a creature shifts, they move as mentioned by the feature without provoking attacks of opportunity.\n## Tactical Docterines\nWhen you get access to stamina, you must choose a tactical docterine to follow. Your chosen tactical docterine gives you **Tactics** which are ways to use your stamina. Currently Available docterines are - **Blademaster**, **Captain** and **Marksman**.\n\nWhen you take a long rest, you may swap your tactical docterine to another.\n## Blademaster\n**Flavor** - A character who has mastered melee weapons.\n### Tactics Learned\n| Name             | Stamina Cost | Action Economy | Type    | Effect Summary                                                                                               |\n| ---------------- | ------------ | -------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| That was a Feint | 1            | On Miss        | Utility | You gain advantage on your next attack if you miss with an attack.                                           |\n| Parry            | 2            | Reaction       | Utility | When an enemy tries to hit you, make an opposing attack roll, if your roll is higher, the attack misses you. |\n| Quick Cut        | 3            | Free Reaction  | Melee   | You make an opportunity attack without using reaction.                                                          |\n| Whirlwind        | 2            | On Hit         | Melee   | You deal damage to all enemies within melee reach                                                            |\n\n## Captain\n**Flavor** - A warrior who controlls the battlefield with the force and wits.\n### Tactics Learned\n| Name             | Stamina Cost | Action Economy | Type    | Effect Summary                                                                                               |\n| ---------------- | ------------ | -------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Demolish         | 2            | Free Action    | Utility | You create difficult terrain around you.                                                                     |\n| Grab             | 2            | On Hit         | Melee   | You grapple an enemy you just hit with a melee attack.                                                       |\n| I am your shield | 2            | Reaction       | Utility | You take the damage intended for another creature.                                                           |\n| Knockback        | 1            | Bonus Action   | Utility | You push a creature.                                                                                         |\n| Reposition       | 2            | Reaction       | Utility | You reposition an ally after an enemy has just moved.                                                        |\n| Rush             | 1            | Bonus Action   | Utility | You take the Dash Action.                                                                                    |\n\n## Marksman\n**Flavor** - A character who has mastered ranged weapons.\n### Tactics Learned\n| Name             | Stamina Cost | Action Economy | Type    | Effect Summary                                                                                               |\n| ---------------- | ------------ | -------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Always Ready     | 3            | Free Reaction  | Utility | You gain temp hp at the start of combat and ignore being surprised.                                          |\n| Hamstring Shot   | 2            | On Hit         | Ranged  | You reduce the speed of your target.                                                                         |\n| Overwatch        | 3            | Free Reaction  | Ranged  | You make an attack against an enemy that just moved.                                                         |\n| Piercing Shot   | 2            | 1 Attack      | Ranged  | You replace one of your attacks with an AOE Line attack.\n\n## Tactics - Detailed View\n### Always Ready\n- **Stamina Cost** - 3\n- **Action Economy** - Free Reaction\n- **Trigger** - Combat Starts\n- **Effect** - You gain 20 temproary HP and remove the suprised condition from yourself.\n## \n### Demolish\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - Free Action\n- **Effect** - Your demolishing might or a prepared explosive charge destroys the terrain around you. Choose a 15ft square on the ground within 5ft of you that area becomes Difficult Terrain. \n### Grab\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - On hit with a melee attack\n- **Effect** - When you Hit with an Melee Weapon Attack or Unarmed Strike, your target is Grappled.\n### Hamstring Shot\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - On hit with a ranged attack\n- **Effect** - When you hit with a ranged weapon attack, you drop the target prone.\n### I am your shield\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - Reaction\n- **Trigger** - A creature within 15 ft of you takes damage\n- **Effect** - You shift adjacent to the creature and take the damage instead. \n### Knockback\n- **Stamina Cost** - 1\n- **Action Economy** - Bonus Action\n- **Target** - One Large or smaller creature or Object\n- **Range** - 5ft\n- **Effect** - Strength Save against Character DC\n- **Failed Save** - The target is pushed horizontally away from you, equal to **5*STR Mod** ft for a minimum of 10 ft. Due to this push if the target impacts another creature, object or structure they take 1d6 bludgeoning damage for each 10 ft of movement that they weren’t pushed.\n- **Made Save** - The target is pushed 5 ft.\n### Overwatch\n- **Stamina Cost** - 3\n- **Action Economy** - Free Reaction\n- **Trigger** - An enemy moves in a position you can see within 60 ft.\n- **Effect** - You make a ranged attack against that creature.\n### Parry\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - Reaction\n- **Trigger** - When you are hit (even a critical hit) by an attack.\n- **Effect** - Make an Attack Roll using a Melee Weapon.\n- **Success** - If your attack roll is equal to or greater than the attack roll made against you, you are not effected by the triggering attack.\n- **Failure** - If your attack roll is lower, the triggering attack hits as normal.\n### Piercing Shot\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - Replaces one of your attacks in the Attack Action while you are wielding a ranged weapon.\n- **Target** - 30ft long, 5ft wide line originating from self.\n- **Effect** - All enemies within the targeted area must make Dex Saves against your Character DC.\n- **Failed Save** - 3d6 Piercing Damage\n- **Made Save** - Half Damage.\n### Quick Cut\n- **Stamina Cost** - 3\n- **Action Economy** - Free Reaction\n- **Trigger** - Whenever you are allowed to make an Attack of Opportunity.\n- **Effect** - You can instead choose to make a Quick Cut. (Which isn’t considered an Attack of Opportunity)\n- **Quick Cut** - Make a Melee Weapon Attack or Unarmed Strike. The attack occurs right before the creature leaves your reach.\n### Reposition\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - Reaction\n- **Trigger** - An enemy you can see stops moving.\n- **Effect** - Choose an Ally that can hear or see you, they can use their reaction to shift up to half their speed.\n### Rush\n- **Stamina Cost** - 1\n- **Action Economy** - Bonus Action\n- **Effect** - You take the Dash Action.\n### That was a Feint\n- **Stamina Cost** - 1\n- **Action Economy** - On Miss\n- **Effect** - When you miss with a melee weapon attack, you gain advantage on your next attack against the same creature.\n### Whirlwind\n- **Stamina Cost** - 2\n- **Action Economy** - On Hit\n- **Effect** - When you hit with a Melee Weapon Attack, you can choose to whirlwind. If you do so, all enemies within your Weapon's Reach take 2*PB Damage. The type of damage is the same as your weapon.\n", "category": "Systems", "frontmatter_yaml": "emoji: \"💪\"\nwip: \"false\"\nsummary: \"System that provides martial characters with extra combat utility.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "a937abbc-cc50-5ce3-8a01-3dedf4e6cb11", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "stamina-system", "status": "published", "title": "Stamina System", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Your persistence in the world is linked with your presence in the community discord server. If for any reason you need to leave the server for a while but intend on returning you can use the following steps to preserve your progress. Whenever you join back all your progress will be restored to you.\n\n**If these steps are not followed when you leave the server, all your progress is lost and characters considered retired.**\n- Open a ticket [here](https://discord.com/channels/1278898468970299523/1278900273804476539/1367600132492754966)\n- Once the ticket is open let us know why you are leaving.\n- Make a note of the current XP, gold, magic items and any other resources for every character you currently have active.\n- If you are a DM make a note of any active storylines you need frozen or handed off.\n- We will take care of the rest.\n", "category": "Conflict & others", "frontmatter_yaml": "emoji: \"👋\"\nwip: \"false\"\nsummary: \"Rules for preserving your progress while you are not in the discord server.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "9a2bb64e-154d-5962-8fc7-890a9c27b952", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "vacation", "status": "published", "title": "Vacation", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "If you are new to the concept of D&D, have only joined here on a whim or merely have heard the name in passing somewhere, worry not here is an entertaining clip that explains the basics of the game in an easy to understand manner under 10 minutes. Let’s watch it together\n> \n[video: Intro to D&D](https://youtu.be/2PEt5RdNHNw?si=RgKupfMPlHVOPG0l)\n\nAnd that really is the core of the game, a conversation. You have a dynamic living world controlled by the dungeon master [DM] in which you can make an infinite amount of choices as your player character [PC].  Obviously like any good game, this simple process is slowly added upon by interesting game mechanics/abilities that make your characters feel like super heroes but the base game-play always remains the same. \n## Traditional D&D and It’s problems\nIf you read the comments of the video above, you will find these as the top two there:\n![Problems with traditional D&D](https://media.discordapp.net/attachments/1553333667349270528/1553663215433678888/image.png?ex=6aba1120&is=6ab8bfa0&hm=7e569859af0a7c260d6b0021267e24314073e68c7a531e2b3f0ac69b808bb0bd&=&format=webp&quality=lossless)\nTraditionally D&D is played in a **3-4 hour** duration, on a table amongst a group of **3-7 players** with one **DM** to direct them. There are games that happen every week on a designated **Game night** and everyone involved is supposed to free this time slot for the game and then be on time, to the designated venue where the game is happening. The nature of D&D is such that every character and their presence is important for the story, similar to a web series, as such if even one player is unable to make it the game usually, has to halt.\n\nIf you have ever scheduled any social gathering you know the pain of finding the correct people and the time at which all of them are able to be together. For something as surreal and experimental as D&D? Well it’s no surprise that many, many people over the world that want to get into this hobby are unable to due to scheduling issues, lack of a venue to play in and a lack of people who share similar interests.\n## Solutions by LiCh\nThis is how LiCh solves these problems to make D&D more accessible than ever.\n| **Problems** | **Solutions** |\n| --- | --- |\n| Whom to play with? | We have a player base of hundreds of players and dozens of **DMs**, so whenever you wish to play you can just request a game and then a group of players and a **DM** who have time in the requested slot will gather around you and you all get to play!  |\n| Where to play? | LC takes D&D online, to play you just join a voice call, like any other group meeting you have experienced over the internet. |\n| When to play? | You play games whenever you have time, LC allows you to participate at your own pace.  |\n\n## Shared World and Connected Story\nDoes every DM create their own world? Do I have to create a new character for every game? What about progress of story and character? Is this just a one shot server? The answer to all these questions is a shared world where all the characters exist and DMs run their adventures. \n\nWe as the server provide the player base with a setting or world. The DMs run all their games in this same world and all player characters exist and progress here. Imagine if you will an MCU like connected story across games and DMs felicitated by the server mechanics and story provided by us. \n## Westmarch Experiences\nWhat can you do in this setting? What can a player do in such a world? How does the flow of D&D change because of the open nature of this world?\n\nWell this approach to D&D where your character is available to you 24/7 and the capability of jumping in and out of their shoes whenever you need makes this style of play almost like a virtual living world. This approach allows for you to do discoveries, inventions, miracles, comedies and tragedies and a lot more. The world lives and breaths right besides our own, rendered in beautiful detail by our DMs and imagination. Instead of a DM holding your hand through a grand adventure of their design. You get to choose what you want to pursue and can do it at your own pace given that world reacts to your actions dynamically.\n", "category": "Getting started", "frontmatter_yaml": "wip: \"false\"\nsummary: \"Introduction to D&D and Westmarches.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "5340646d-c4b6-5ceb-beea-1bf8202d8a50", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "welcome-to-d-d", "status": "published", "title": "Welcome to D&D", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "So hiding is a staple of this game, let's clarify how this works in the 2024 rules.\n## Hide - Action\n### What does this action do?\nIt gives a creature the Invisible condition.\n### Prerequisites?\nTo be able to take the hide action you need to meet two conditions.\n- **Cover**: You need to be either heavily obscured or have three quarters or full cover. [ DM adjudicates whether you have cover or not.]\n- **Break LOS**: You need to break line of sight with **all** enemies around you. If you can see a creature, you can discern whether or not they can see you. [ DM adjudicates whether or not you have broken LOS]\n### What to do once we have met the prerequisites?\n- You need to make a Stealth (DEX) check, the minimum this check needs to be for you to successfully hide is 15.\n- Make a note of your check's total, this is the DC for perception checks others can make to find you.\n### When do the benefits of a hide action end?\nWhenever you make a sound louder than a whisper, an enemy finds you, you make an attack roll, or you cast a spell with a Verbal component.\n### Passive perception\nIf you are attempting to hide and one of the enemies whose senses can reach your position has higher passive perception than your stealth total, you fail to hide.\n\n**Example:** A goblin has 60 ft. of dark vision and a passive perception of 16. If you attempt to hide within 60 ft. of it and only roll a 15, your attempt to hide fails.\n\n## Sound\nSo, the audible distance of sound in D&D has two components, **Noise** and **Distance**.\n- Noise is how loud the creature who is making the sound wants it to be. The intention.\n- Distance is the number in feet the sound actually travels, this is related to the level of noise the creature had intended to make.\n- There are three tiers that the audible distance is divided into\n\n![Audible Distance](https://i.ibb.co/WvF0Rx7s/Audible-Distance.webp)\n\n> If a PC is the one making sound, the DM can either roll the for distance the sound travelled themselves or ask the player to do so based on their intended loudness.\nIf you are hiding from an enemy and the enemy hears you, it does break your invisible condition granted by the hide action.\n## Visibility\nNow this one is a lot more nuanced. Visibility is affected by the geography of where the viewer is. I know, big surprise.\n### Visibility - Outdoors.\nWhen traveling outdoors, most characters can see about 2 miles in any direction on a clear day, except where obstructions block their view. That range increases to 40 miles if they are atop a mountain or a tall hill or are otherwise able to look down on the area from a height. Lightly Obscured conditions reduce visibility: rain reduces maximum visibility to 1 mile, and fog reduces it to between 100 and 300 feet.\n\nThis is the general visibility of the location and environment, noticing specific creatures however triggers encounters. The distance at which you can trigger encounters is affected further by the type of terrain you are in, generally it is the DM who determines the distance of an encounter and translates it for the players.\n\n![Travel Terrain](https://i.ibb.co/1fs1fXX5/Travel-Terrain.webp)\n\n> **Example:** If the adventure is happening in coastal region, the adventuring party would only notice the water elemental floating in the sea, when they are at least within 100 ft of it.\n### Visibility - Sea\nFrom a ship’s crow’s nest, a lookout can see things up to 10 miles away, assuming clear skies and a relatively calm sea. Overcast skies reduce that distance by half. Lightly Obscured conditions reduce visibility just as they do on land.\n### Visibility - Underwater\nVisibility underwater depends on water clarity and the available light. Use the Underwater Encounter Distance table to determine the encounter distances underwater.\n\n![Underwater Encounter Distance](https://i.ibb.co/JW7yQFYq/Underwater-Encounter-Distance.webp)\n", "category": "ruling", "frontmatter_yaml": "tagKey: \"clarify\"\ntagLabel: \"Rules Clarification\"\nsummary: \"How hiding, sound and visibility work in D&D 5.5e.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "ad2ae736-8f14-5edc-abc6-2b9c519bb691", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "hiding", "status": "published", "title": "Hiding", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "If an object is anchor for a casting of a the darkness spell, then when the object is moved the spell ends.\n", "category": "ruling", "frontmatter_yaml": "tagKey: \"ruling\"\ntagLabel: \"Rules Clarification\"\nsummary: \"If an object with darkness cast upon it is moved, the spell on it ends.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "afb8a3ce-cda0-5852-aa76-b2a7fe6b83e5", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "moving-darkness", "status": "published", "title": "Moving Darkness", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "- Must be of a CR 0 stat block, 'Beast' from an official source book.\n- You are free to describe how you found the pet.\n- Must spend 25GP. This takes into consideration the upkeep for their livelihood.\n- You can take any number of your pets into session but they do not take turns in combat, or provide any mechanical benefit beyond being a cute presence by your side.\n- If it goes into a session, treat it fairly. This goes to both, Players and DMs.\n- If you (the DM) wish not to harm a pet in your session, do not allow for the pet to be taken into the session in the first place. Players to also check in with the DMs when signing up or at the start of game at the latest so both parties are on the same page.\n", "category": "ruling", "frontmatter_yaml": "tagKey: \"guide\"\ntagLabel: \"Rules Clarification\"\nsummary: \"Rules about non-mechanical pets.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "1d2d1ed4-e896-52c5-85ba-b91ec2919f56", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "pets", "status": "published", "title": "Pets", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["slug"], "refs": {"author_id": {"platform_owner": true, "table": "members"}}, "row": {"body": "Many spells in the game grant temporary hp as a part of their effects, offering lingering effects related to the spell that are associated with the temp hp. Example - Armor of Agathys, Polymorph.\nFor such spells only the Temporary HP granted by the spell itself is counted for the associated spell effect. Secondly, the temporary HP given out by such spells goes away when the spell granting them ends for whatever reason.\n", "category": "ruling", "frontmatter_yaml": "tagKey: \"ruling\"\ntagLabel: \"Rules Clarification\"\nsummary: \"Temp HP granted by spells goes away when the spell duration ends.\"\n", "kind": "rule", "public_id": "cc2bdf3f-dbe6-572f-accd-dbe57f9fe746", "published_at": "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z", "slug": "temp-hp-spells", "status": "published", "title": "Temp HP & Spells", "version": 1, "visibility": "public"}, "table": "documents"}
{"conflict": ["public_id"], "refs": {"document_id": {"public_id": "e291c9d3-5294-5099-b34a-47370e9bb9d9", "table": "documents"}}, "row": {"kind": "server", "name": "Lightfall", "public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14"}, "table": "regions"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_humans", "description": "Humans are the most common species in Weeping, and their ability to train and develop resistance to the cursed rains has earned them key positions in the Hierocracy. Legend says that humans from across the realms travelled to Weeping during the Age of Heroes.", "kind": "species", "name": "Humans", "payload": {"also_called": "Anthropoi", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md", "today_in_lightfall": "Humans of all ehnicity roam lightfall, they fill most seats of the Temple, the Inquisition, and the council beneath the empty Agora."}, "public_id": "2bfb83f5-0e0a-5958-8519-a584a9705606", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_dwarves", "description": "Living longer than humans, these sturdy folk spend most of their lives devoted to metal or stone craft. Many feel a natural calling to create one masterpiece from the earth itself.", "kind": "species", "name": "Dwarves", "payload": {"also_called": "Daktyloi", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md", "today_in_lightfall": "You'll find them in the Crafter's District, working stone and iron for the city they helped build."}, "public_id": "992323c3-06f6-5de8-ba00-f772fb24774e", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_metalborn_dwarves", "description": "After generations spent mastering the forge, this subspecies of dwarves was blessed by Hephaestus, god of the forge. They embody the forge itself, and their metallic bodies serve as their own shields.", "kind": "species", "name": "Metalborn Dwarves", "payload": {"also_called": "Khalkeoi", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md", "today_in_lightfall": "Their gleaming frames are a common sight at the forges and among the Emberwatch's ranks."}, "public_id": "d24e3c79-109d-5429-85b9-66b393f79da9", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_orcs", "description": "Most orcs trace their origins to the desert of the far east, where they lived as nomads. Many mark themselves with tattoos and body paints that show their social standing and achievements.", "kind": "species", "name": "Orcs", "payload": {"also_called": "Skythai", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md", "today_in_lightfall": "Their painted marks are easy to spot, helping and carrying the city's markets, where the old nomad habit of travelling light persists."}, "public_id": "9d9c4963-cf10-579d-92eb-6ac8d494e1d9", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_gnomes", "description": "Gnomes are a curious people forever chasing their own beginnings. Their oldest records say they were found at the end of a mechanical labyrinth, and every gnome since has been a little bit of a maze-walker, collecting clues, keeping threads, and cheerfully refusing to stop asking.", "kind": "species", "name": "Gnomes", "payload": {"also_called": "Daidalidai", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "ab67e9be-8cd3-5b58-969a-636fe9ef6d03", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_halflings", "description": "Halflings were the first farmers to emerge after the weeping began. These peace-loving folk love to nurture and till the land through the divine channels, and they rarely leave the farmlands. They are also very particular about time and punctuality.", "kind": "species", "name": "Halflings", "payload": {"also_called": "Horaioi", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md", "today_in_lightfall": "They tend the fields beyond the wall, and it's said the Granary's deliveries arrive on the hour, to the minute."}, "public_id": "5f002166-d490-5455-8890-9766ebe3cf26", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_tieflings", "description": "Descendants of the black desert, these half-demons are known for using steam and magic to power through their foes and daily life. The hotblooded theifling sociert measures status by how richly their horns and tails are decorated.", "kind": "species", "name": "Tieflings", "payload": {"also_called": "Kerastai", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md", "today_in_lightfall": "Their steam-driven craft is welcome in the workshops, though the Metron watches their magic closely."}, "public_id": "c92ce1e8-0708-5c2d-a1c3-583008dcb481", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_aasimar", "description": "The aasimar descend from an unknown angel that once came down to the northern wastelands. These divine-blooded people live in tribal societies and yearn to understand and follow divine will.", "kind": "species", "name": "Aasimar", "payload": {"also_called": "Ouranidai", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md", "today_in_lightfall": "Many find a calling in the Temple and in Sol Invictus, serving a light they feel they were born to understand."}, "public_id": "169e6cd9-ac9f-57cc-88ea-564dffb1f102", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_dragonborn", "description": "The dragonborn hail from the eastern volcanic islands, where tradition is bound to honor, blade, and breath. They were once revered for being an elite hunting force slaying monsters the size of mountains.", "kind": "species", "name": "Dragonborn", "payload": {"also_called": "Spartoi", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "c39953e6-ebf1-523b-92e6-effc0ba1f830", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_goliath", "description": "These half-giants mark their clans with different body paints. Each clan holds the others responsible for a grudge so ancient that no one remembers its cause.", "kind": "species", "name": "Goliath", "payload": {"also_called": "Gigantes", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "33908fa6-eccd-5ad3-a8fe-75eef5d95db0", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_cyclops", "description": "Considered descendants of evil, the cyclopes were once subjugated during the Age of Heroes and forced to fight against evil. Only after the weeping began were they welcomed back into society.", "kind": "species", "name": "Cyclops", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "ddab0775-7023-5fe9-ab69-6eaaa9300cb3", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_automa", "description": "The automa are creations of divine will. Their bronze-and-ceramic bodies are often linked to many different gods, but scholars have yet to reach a consensus on their origins. Most are found in ruins older than the Age of Heroes.", "kind": "species", "name": "Automa", "payload": {"also_called": "Warforged", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "01c2d5b8-5b24-5055-9716-0023b0916b54", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_aphotics", "description": "The lightless ones were once hunted as blood drinkers. When the clouds of the weeping covered the sun, they became the greatest allies of humankind. After aiding the Lightkeeper on divine quests, they were finally accepted in parts of the city, and today they walk the shadowy alleyways, tolerated by the Inquisition.", "kind": "species", "name": "Aphotics", "payload": {"also_called": "Dhampir", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "87f1b51a-430a-5ce0-ab9e-fe24d60ad56a", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_lykaonoi", "description": "Once believed lost to time, these warrior canines were found beneath the weeping sky in a surprisingly human form. Some succumb to a rabid urge on the brightest nights, so the species is closely monitored by the Metron.", "kind": "species", "name": "Lykaonoi", "payload": {"also_called": "Lupin", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "d164bd79-33db-509d-8cf0-b3d9cc616da1", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_baskanoi", "description": "The baskanoi wear crowns of wood that glow with an eerie light. Once lost to wild magic, they were brought back under control by the light of Apollo and given a second chance in civilization because of their resistance to the weeping.", "kind": "species", "name": "Baskanoi", "payload": {"also_called": "Hexblood", "edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Species in Weeping.md"}, "public_id": "142b3be1-5ed6-57b5-98d4-2f324eb98523", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_artificer", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Artificer", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Alchemist", "touchpoint": "The old art of turning one thing into another, handed down from sages who sought the secret of gold and the cure for every ill."}, {"name": "Armorer", "touchpoint": "The forge-god's craft, which taught that bronze can be taught to stand like a hero. Armor remembers the wearers who came before."}, {"name": "Artillerist", "touchpoint": "Fire and thunder bound into a tube of metal, in the spirit of the gods' own bolts. Some say the first design fell from the sky."}, {"name": "Battle Smith", "touchpoint": "A craft that breathes will into metal, the way golden hands once served at a divine forge. Steel follows, and sometimes it chooses to follow its own will."}, {"name": "Cartographer", "touchpoint": "Mapmakers are employed in service of ember watch, charting outer roads, recording where light holds and where it falls."}, {"name": "Reanimator", "touchpoint": "Certain Licensed Anatomist apply the craft to heal and restore under the Metron guild and Pallor House."}]}, "public_id": "a94f7f90-3748-58f7-825d-f996ca7488fa", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_barbarian", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Barbarian", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Berserker", "touchpoint": "A fury like the heroes of old, who fought until the field was quiet and the battle-madness left them. It rises when the rain does."}, {"name": "Wild Heart", "touchpoint": "The old kinship between warriors and beasts, as in the tales of lions, boars, and bulls. The wild still knows its own."}, {"name": "World Tree", "touchpoint": "Strength drawn from being a part of the great tree's roots, the ones most connected to the light."}, {"name": "Zealot", "touchpoint": "Holy fury aimed at the cursed, whether Apollo's or an Olympian's, they are watched warily by the Temple."}]}, "public_id": "ba85e186-d7d6-5bb5-bbab-7075c7084750", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_bard", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Bard", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Dance", "touchpoint": "The first steps taken before the first temple, when worship was movement. The body remembers what the mind forgot."}, {"name": "Glamour", "touchpoint": "Charm developed by working on noble salons and Old God devotees, called a blessing by some and a snare by others."}, {"name": "Lore", "touchpoint": "The long thread of songs, tales, and half-remembered verses. Every story tells a little of the truth, and the bard keeps them all."}, {"name": "Valor", "touchpoint": "The courage in a marching song, and the vow that carries a lantern into the dark. It lifts those spirits of those out in weeping away from light."}, {"name": "Moon", "touchpoint": "The silver goddess behind the clouds, still listening. Her stories are told in the dark, where the herds sleep."}, {"name": "Spirit", "touchpoint": "The voices of heroes and the restless dead, who linger because a song is still unfinished. Summoning one is asking a favor from the past."}]}, "public_id": "4b20efb2-b00b-53e0-ae78-769c12fdc107", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_cleric", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Cleric", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Life", "touchpoint": "The warmth of the harvest and the hearth, healing from the Temple's almshouses and Demeter-blessed field clans."}, {"name": "Light", "touchpoint": "The god of the sun and the song, shining through whoever will carry it. A flame passed from hand to hand."}, {"name": "Trickery", "touchpoint": "The laugh of the wanderer and the wine-god's mischief. Rules bend, and a clever tongue is a holy tool."}, {"name": "War", "touchpoint": "The roar of the old war-gods and the discipline of the phalanx. A faith that expects its faithful to stand."}, {"name": "Knowledge", "touchpoint": "The oracle's hunger for truth. Every answer is a candle, and every candle shows another door."}, {"name": "Grave", "touchpoint": "The ferryman's patient mercy. The dead deserve rest, and the faithful see that they get it."}, {"name": "Arcana", "touchpoint": "Priests licensed by the Metron, who treat magic as a gift to be measured and used in service of the light."}]}, "public_id": "1df6f14b-1cfb-523d-bc68-95468ebd8a7f", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_druid", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Druid", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Land", "touchpoint": "The soil, the grove, and the green that remembers the light which brough it to life under the weeping sky."}, {"name": "Moon", "touchpoint": "The moon's shapes, remembered when she is hidden. A druid takes the form of whatever the luna asks for."}, {"name": "Sea", "touchpoint": "The deep water and the creatures of myth, worshipped on far eastern shores. Tide and storm obey those who learn their names."}, {"name": "Stars", "touchpoint": "The slow wheel of the heavens, read through gaps in the cloud. Every light overhead is an old story told in fire."}]}, "public_id": "1ca48de7-6992-5d92-b453-38885be6557a", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_fighter", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Fighter", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Arcane archer", "touchpoint": "A shot that carries a spell with it, as the old archers were said to carry the gods' favor but now are embewed with arcane enhancements."}, {"name": "Banneret", "touchpoint": "Standard-bearers of the Emberwatch's road-companies and the Frostmarch garrison, trained in the art of revitalizing and rallying others with the colors of the light."}, {"name": "Battle master", "touchpoint": "The wisdom of old heroic strategists and the drill of the phalanx. A battle is a puzzle, and the master knows the shape of every piece."}, {"name": "Champion", "touchpoint": "Renown earned in the Sand Court competitions and by raw competence on the outer roads bring in the excellence, the body made perfect and a name worth singing"}, {"name": "Eldritch Knight", "touchpoint": "Steel and spellcraft learned under a Metron-licensed tutor in the light of heroes that once walked between mortal crafts and divne gifts."}, {"name": "Psi Warrior", "touchpoint": "Power honed within the self, this disciplined will was trained in the halls or hardened against the dread of the weeping."}]}, "public_id": "13bbe60c-9a72-584d-9e51-dcc331ba344a", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_monk", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Monk", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Mercy", "touchpoint": "The hand that heals and the hand that ends, both guided by the same compassion. A quiet vow to the divines to ease suffering by any means."}, {"name": "Shadow", "touchpoint": "Martial artists lurking in shadowed Quarter's alley and the underground chambers of the city"}, {"name": "Mystic arts", "touchpoint": "Gymnasion-trained fighters who pair martial discipline with licensed spellcraft, a rare blend of Temple focus and Metron craft."}, {"name": "Elements", "touchpoint": "Deciples of westside monasteries and Metalborn forge-halls that study fire, stone, and wind."}, {"name": "Open Hand", "touchpoint": "Born of unarmed discipline from the Gymnasion's wrestlers or the Temple's silent cloisters."}]}, "public_id": "b044b403-ae5e-52fe-999a-564e3b8fac2b", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_paladin", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Paladin", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Devotion", "touchpoint": "Warriors of divine bronze with vows to Apollo, the Lightkeeper, and the Temple."}, {"name": "Glory", "touchpoint": "Seekers of deeds worthy of a hymn, in the arena or beyond the borders of light."}, {"name": "Ancients", "touchpoint": "Guardians of the fields and of a light an order older than the tree, Older than the rains.. their existence.. their heroics. kept quiet."}, {"name": "Noble Genies", "touchpoint": "No genies walk Weeping, so this oath follows the four elemental spirits of earth, air, fire, and water, honored by the southern Band's caravans and the eastern volcanic islands."}, {"name": "Vengance", "touchpoint": "Hunters of the cursed and enforcers of the Laws of the Light against those who profit from the weeping."}]}, "public_id": "01cbd5fc-b592-5e9b-a20d-e2f84a72fe0e", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_ranger", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Ranger", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Beast Master", "touchpoint": "Warriors of the land with companions trained beside Emberwatch hounds or chosen from the wild edge."}, {"name": "Fey Wanderer", "touchpoint": "Warriors channeling magic from strange presences at the light's edge and minor Greek spirits of spring and grove."}, {"name": "Gloom Stalker", "touchpoint": "Hunters in the dark beyond the towers and the drains beneath the city."}, {"name": "Hollow warden", "touchpoint": "Monstrous guardians who draw on the ancient terrors of the jagged mountains and cracked cliff's shadows ringing the realm."}, {"name": "Hunter", "touchpoint": "Road-scouts and monster-slayers who study the cursed and trade notes at the Bestiary Hall."}, {"name": "Winter warden", "touchpoint": "Frostmarch hunters in the northern wastes, regarded with respect and fear for the cold they carry back."}]}, "public_id": "080818a4-c4a1-5d1c-bd8f-2c0727ced18d", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_rogue", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Rogue", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Arcane Trickster", "touchpoint": "These tricksters are tolerated by the Metron, often called Festive illusionists."}, {"name": "Assassin", "touchpoint": "Quiet blades for hire in the Veil Market, or silent hands of the Order of Frostmarch."}, {"name": "Soulknife", "touchpoint": "Rogues that wield their mind that glows faintly with faith or shines with pure will."}, {"name": "Thief", "touchpoint": "Rooftop runners and relic-lifters who slip into ruins and private collections."}, {"name": "Scion of the three", "touchpoint": "Agents of three dark Olympians, Ares (slaughter), Hades (death), and Kratos (tyranny), tolerated by no one and worshipped in the cellars of the Veil Market"}, {"name": "Phantom", "touchpoint": "Spies and information-gatherers who walk the line between life and death, prized by the Shadowed Quarter's back-alley brokers."}]}, "public_id": "09cbf646-cfce-5373-aeb9-c87baed18196", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_sorcerer", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Sorcerer", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Aberrant", "touchpoint": "A whisper from the deep dark before the age of heroes, old like the Titans and strange to every hymn. It seeps through the rain and falls from the ring, and some blood answers."}, {"name": "Clockwork", "touchpoint": "A quiet order of bronze and ichor, kin to the forge-god's golden hands and the giant who walked the shores. Gnomes and automa hum with the same old rhythm."}, {"name": "Draconic", "touchpoint": "The memory of serpents slain by heroes, and of teeth sown into the earth that rose as warriors. Some blood still carries that old fire."}, {"name": "Wild Magic", "touchpoint": "The laughter and discord of revels no god quite rules. It surges where it wishes and answers to no hymn, no guild, and no law."}, {"name": "Spellfire", "touchpoint": "A spark of the sun-god's own radiance, or of fire stolen from the heavens. The Temple calls it a gift, and the Metron calls it a danger."}, {"name": "Shadow", "touchpoint": "The first night, folded into the dark beyond the tree's glow. It gathers where the lamps thin, and it knows those who walk there."}]}, "public_id": "7537bed5-d2e6-5580-809f-d36bb71b1cf3", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_warlock", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Warlock", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Archfey", "touchpoint": "A spirit of spring, grove, or crossroads, half-remembered from the days before the rain. It laughs at the light's edge and bargains in riddles, and it is never quite what it seems.."}, {"name": "Celestial", "touchpoint": "A radiant presence that answers from the sun's own halls. It might be a herald of the god of light, or a fallen winged thing from beyond the north that still remembers the glow."}, {"name": "Fiend", "touchpoint": "A voice from the black desert's old fires or from the deep pit beneath the world, where the dead and the damned are weighed. It always offers a fair deal, and the cost comes later."}, {"name": "Great old one", "touchpoint": "Something that watched before the gods had names. It might wait behind the ring, or hide in the storm, and it speaks in a tongue the hymns never learned."}, {"name": "Undead", "touchpoint": "A mind that walked out of the grave with its memories intact. It might be a hero who refused the river of forgetting, or a patient shade of the night that tires of waiting in the dark."}, {"name": "Vestige", "touchpoint": "A god whose temples have fallen to dust and whose name the rain has worn smooth. It might be a forgotten Olympian, or something far older, still hungry to be remembered."}]}, "public_id": "729b0281-5b38-5322-8354-700539a92bc9", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_wizard", "description": null, "kind": "class", "name": "Wizard", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Classes.md", "subclasses": [{"name": "Abjurer", "touchpoint": "Wards for the cleansing towers and the city's gates"}, {"name": "Diviner", "touchpoint": "Readers of the rain's pattern and portents in the ring and stars."}, {"name": "Evoker", "touchpoint": "Fire and force under Metron permit, or sharpened against fallen angels"}, {"name": "Illusionist", "touchpoint": "Light-weaving that borrows the tree's glow, and spectacle for holy days."}, {"name": "Conjurer", "touchpoint": "Summoners of objects and creatures, answering to the Metron's strict register."}, {"name": "Enchanter", "touchpoint": "Charm-workers of the noble salons and Rhapsodes' stages, watched for how freely they bend a will"}, {"name": "Necromancer", "touchpoint": "Metron-licensed students of death, always asked how their craft differs from the curse."}, {"name": "Transmuter", "touchpoint": "Reshapers of matter in the Crafter's District and the light-wrights' benches."}]}, "public_id": "c77b3b04-85d5-5607-a12a-d40a91f75d27", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_acolyte", "description": "Variants: Order of the Light Ward, Hearth keeper of the old gods", "kind": "background", "name": "Acolyte", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You walked into the Temple's healing halls and asked to be useful. Maybe you wanted to repay the care you received, or maybe the sight of the sick pulled you in. Your days ran by the dawn and dusk prayers, with the hours between spent grinding herbs, boiling bandages, and sitting with the sick. You learned that the healers keep careful records, and that people carried in from the curse of weeping are rarely leave.", "name": "Order of the Light Ward", "tags": ["Secluded", "Bureaucratic", "Academic"]}, {"blurb": "You heard Olympian devotees mentioned in passing and went looking for them, curious about gods you couldn't name but felt you should know. They were not hard to find. Their shrines sit in plain sight: a small altar in the corner of a shop, a niche at a crossroads, a hearth with a flame kept just for them. They welcomed you and asked if you would keep one. You learned the old rites: wine poured at dusk, names spoken aloud, small offerings left at the foot of a statue. Nothing about it is forbidden, but the city has opinions. Apollo's light is expected to come first, and a devotee who forgets that will feel the cooling of a neighbor's smile and the careful silence of the Temple. Most keepers simply open their evening prayers with a nod to the tree, then turn to their own gods, and no one minds. Your circle shares the work: someone tends the flame, someone teaches the old songs, and someone listens for how the district is feeling.", "name": "Hearth keeper of the old gods", "tags": ["Secluded", "Anarchic", "Academic"]}]}, "public_id": "9cdf7715-7534-5d2b-94bd-a22aa2c03b96", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_artisan", "description": "Variants: Crafter's Guild Apprentice, Light-wright's Bench", "kind": "background", "name": "Artisan", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You visited the Crafter's District to see whether your hands remembered anything, and a guildmaster handed you a tool. They did. You asked to be taken on and moved through apprenticeship faster than anyone thought possible. Your trade might be smithing, armoring, light-wrighting, herbalism, or alchemy. The guild, shaped under Távros, tests every apprentice and stamps every finished piece with a maker's mark. For someone with no past, that mark was a rare comfort: here you could prove who you were by what you made.", "name": "Crafter's Guild Apprentice", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Creative"]}, {"blurb": "You went to the light-wrights because you were drawn to the glow. Light-wrights shape lanterns, mirrors, and lenses, and their work is closer to prayer than to trade. The light they work with is not fire. It is a fragment of Apollo's own radiance, the same warm and steady light that fell from the tree, and a light-wright is its caretaker, not its maker. Every lantern is blessed at the Temple before it leaves the bench, and every mirror carries a priest's mark of approval. A lantern for a cleansing tower may be blessed by the Temple's priests, then guarded on the road to its post. Your master taught you that light is never to be wasted, hoarded, or sold as an ordinary good. Apprentices learn to grind glass, set reflective bronze plates, and keep their hands steady while the divine light warms the metal. You spend your days in the quiet glow of the workshop, and you have noticed that your work shines brighter than it should.", "name": "Light-wright's Bench", "tags": ["Secluded", "Communal", "Creative"]}]}, "public_id": "a41ac7ad-4991-5477-8a9e-68301aed27b9", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_charlatan", "description": "Variants: Counterfeit Oracle, Relic Forger", "kind": "background", "name": "Charlatan", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You discovered that people with no past are very good at pretending to see the future. On the clear days, when the clouds thin and the ring floats overhead, you set up on a street corner and read omens for coin. You told farmers about their harvests, merchants about their fortunes, and the lovelorn about whatever they wanted to hear. The Temple's weather-seers read the true divine pattern and you read only faces, but your customers don't know the difference. You've learned to watch for a tremor in someone's voice, a glance at a neighbor, a coin counted twice. The work is quiet and a little shameful, but you are good at it, and some part of you hopes you might someday be right by accident.", "name": "Counterfeit Oracle", "tags": ["Urban", "Anarchic", "Lawless"]}, {"blurb": "You noticed that collectors pay well for pieces from before the Age of Heroes, and that most of them never check the stonework. So you started to make your own. You carve inscriptions in half-forgotten scripts, age bronze with salts and smoke, and pass off the results as ruin finds. Your forgeries sell to nobles, scholars, and the occasional Temple priest who wants a curiosity for a shelf. You learned that forging is a craft in its own right, with tools, methods, and a fierce pride in doing it well. You also learned to avoid the Metron's appraisers and the Ruin-delvers who know the real thing. The work taught you enough about ancient craft to fool experts, and to wonder how you learned it so quickly.", "name": "Relic Forger", "tags": ["Urban", "Anarchic", "Creative"]}]}, "public_id": "8426f8ca-bbb8-5f44-b6b4-75d203f4994c", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_criminal", "description": "Variants: Criminal Gang, Cult Courier", "kind": "background", "name": "Criminal", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You went looking for work that asked no questions, and one night someone offered you a job. The organization is not a gang of cutpurses. It is a quiet, well-run enterprise that moves ██████ through the Granary's back doors, lends coin to the desperate at terms nobody reads, and keeps a ledger as careful as the Inquisition's. You know you were paid on the first of every month. You know you were told never to carry anything yourself. Beyond that, the details have gone soft, like a dream in the afternoon. You remember a room with ████████, a man whose face you can't recall, and a rule you were taught on your first day: ██████████. Some people say the organization has a way of making leavers forget. Others say it is only that no one wants to remember.", "name": "Criminal Gang", "tags": ["Urban", "Communal", "Lawless"]}, {"blurb": "You were curious about the whispers that the weeping is a judgment, and a stranger at a market stall offered you coin to carry a parcel across the city. You never opened one. You learned the routes: a baker's back door, a cobbler's shuttered window, a laundry line with a particular knot. The believers speak softly and mean what they say, and some of them are neighbors you have bought bread from. You never decided whether you agreed with them, and the not-knowing is what keeps you in the work.", "name": "Cult Courier", "tags": ["Urban", "Anarchic", "Lawless"]}]}, "public_id": "a67ba23d-6102-596d-b19c-65e89b8c7317", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_entertainer", "description": "Variants: Temple Chorus, Gymnasion Showman", "kind": "background", "name": "Entertainer", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You were drawn to the dawn and dusk services by the singing, and one morning you found yourself joining in. The choirmaster noticed and asked you to stay. You learned the hymns, the breath control, and the quiet discipline of singing in unison with forty strangers while the sun comes up. The old melodies come out of your mouth before you can think of them, and a few of the older singers have started watching you.", "name": "Temple Chorus", "tags": ["Urban", "Communal", "Creative"]}, {"blurb": "You went to the public halls to see what your body could do, and found that crowds gather when it does it well. You wrestle, race, and spar in the open courts, and the audience cheers on festival days and tavern nights alike. You learned showmanship: when to pull a punch, when to let the other fighter win, and how to land a throw so the whole hall gasps. Recruiters from the Emberwatch and the Order of Frostmarch watch from the benches, and you've learned to pretend not to notice.", "name": "Gymnasion Showman", "tags": ["Urban", "Communal", "Martial"]}]}, "public_id": "6be22866-6cfc-5f31-94cb-d2f601b80061", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_farmer", "description": "Variants: Channel-tenders and Orchard Keepers, Herd-hand", "kind": "background", "name": "Farmer", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You left the walls for the fields, wanting space and quiet. A halfling clan agreed to take you on, and you were given a shovel, a timepiece, and a pair of pruning shears. By day you open and close the divine channels on the minute; by season you prune and graft in the groves beyond the wall. You learned that water and fruit both reward patience and punctuality. Few outsiders have worked beside the clan, and you were one of them.", "name": "Channel-tenders and Orchard Keepers", "tags": ["Rural", "Communal", "Labor"]}, {"blurb": "Rural - Communal - Labor", "name": "Herd-hand", "tags": []}]}, "public_id": "c32a0cb7-2cb2-5577-8149-92dba1d2c4df", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_guard", "description": "Variants: Tower Sentry, City Guard", "kind": "background", "name": "Guard", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You asked for a post at a cleansing tower and were given a spear, a lantern, and a shift roster. You stand watch on the boundary, guarding the flame and whoever runs toward it out of the dark. The routine is strict: hours are logged, signals are tested, and no one leaves the tower unmanned. You learned what the weeping looks like when it moves, and how still a person can become when it does.", "name": "Tower Sentry", "tags": ["Secluded", "Bureaucratic", "Martial"]}, {"blurb": "You took a post on the city's streets, walking a beat between the Crafter's District and the Agora. You learned the faces of the regulars, the habits of the markets, and the difference between a loud argument and a dangerous one. Most days are quiet. The work is mostly settling disputes, escorting drunks home, and being seen. You answer to the city's own watch, not the Inquisition, though you learned quickly when to step aside for them.", "name": "City Guard", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Martial"]}]}, "public_id": "7d674f72-576b-5349-b7a1-d3e3f511e6dd", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_guide", "description": "Variants: Pilgrim's Escort, Emberwatch Road-Scout", "kind": "background", "name": "Guide", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You offered your sword to the devout who walk to the boundary shrines, and a pilgrim's guild took you on. You lead small groups along the outer paths, watching for danger and keeping the pace for the slowest walker. You learned that pilgrims come for many reasons: grief, thanks, penance, doubt. You learned which of them are actually ready for the walk, and how to turn back the ones who are not.", "name": "Pilgrim's Escort", "tags": ["Rural", "Communal", "Martial"]}, {"blurb": "Wilderness - Communal - Martial", "name": "Emberwatch Road-Scout", "tags": []}]}, "public_id": "9759a18d-1b52-5322-b558-913fac4885fc", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_hermit", "description": "Variants: Field Hermit, Cloistered Reader", "kind": "background", "name": "Hermit", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You took over a herder's hut at the edge of the fields, where the tree's light is faint and the dark is close. No one assigned it to you and no one objected. You live simply, tend a small garden, and spend your evenings watching the horizon and trying to remember. Farmers leave bread at your door and ask for no company in return. In the silence you have started to notice patterns in the dark that you cannot explain.", "name": "Field Hermit", "tags": ["Rural", "Anarchic", "Academic"]}, {"blurb": "You asked the Temple for a cell and a stack of books, and they gave you both. You spend months copying one prayer after another, waiting for something to feel familiar. The scriptorium keeps strict hours, with silence after the dusk bell and fasting on holy days. You learned the old scripts, the art of illumination, and the careful way a page is read. Most of what you copy is routine, but now and then a line catches in your mind like a hook.", "name": "Cloistered Reader", "tags": ["Secluded", "Bureaucratic", "Academic"]}]}, "public_id": "af00ff27-96b0-5c14-823b-eb51e9fd04e5", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_merchant", "description": "Variants: Plaza Stallkeeper, Grain Factor", "kind": "background", "name": "Merchant", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You rented a stall near the Crafter's District obelisk, wanting honest work where you could watch people come and go. You sell what others make: lamps, ribbons, tools, small charms. Stallkeepers share a loose agreement about who sets up where, who minds whose goods during prayers, and who splits the cost of a bad day. You learned to haggle gently, to judge a customer in a glance, and to tell when a buyer is really looking for something else. Most of the city passes your stall eventually, and many people talk to you.", "name": "Plaza Stallkeeper", "tags": ["Urban", "Communal", "Creative"]}, {"blurb": "You took a post as a go-between for the farms and the Granary of Aetós, wanting steady work with numbers and a view of the city's food supply. You weigh samples, negotiate prices, and settle disputes between halfling clans and granary clerks. You learned the rhythm of the harvest: which fields are heavy, which are failing, and which families are quietly short. The Granary's books must balance, and you are the person who makes them.", "name": "Grain Factor", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Labor"]}]}, "public_id": "dc9b8997-14aa-5e07-8f5c-567cfb954c66", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_noble", "description": "Variants: Noble Household Retainer, Council Page", "kind": "background", "name": "Noble", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You applied for a post with a noble house, hoping for security and a view of how the city is run. The house hired you as an aide, curious about a Solarii with no history. You ate well, learned etiquette, and attended gatherings where the Noble Houses weigh the city's affairs. You were never a guest and never quite a servant, and you learned to read the difference in every glance.", "name": "Noble Household Retainer", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Noble"]}, {"blurb": "You carry messages for a noble house's representative at the council, running between the Agora, the Temple, the Inquisition, and the Metron. You learned the council's halls, its unwritten rules, and which doors open for a young face in house colors. Pages hear things. Nobody minds, because pages are expected to forget what they hear. You have learned how often the empty Agora comes up in conversation, and how carefully people avoid the question of who will fill it.", "name": "Council Page", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Noble"]}]}, "public_id": "612af881-8eb4-5317-acde-168570f6afc2", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_sage", "description": "Variants: Philosophers' Conclave, Metron Archive Reader", "kind": "background", "name": "Sage", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You walked into the public halls because you wanted answers about the weeping, the tree, and the ring. The thinkers there argue about all three, and with no past to defend, you made an ideal debating partner. You learned to build an argument, hold a position, and let go of it gracefully. Rivalries are sharp, and your lack of a history made you oddly welcome to every faction.", "name": "Philosophers' Conclave", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Academic"]}, {"blurb": "You asked to study the Mage's Guild's stacks, and the Metron handed you a reader's token and a very small room. You learned how the archive is organized, which shelves are open, and which are kept behind locked doors. Readers sign for every scroll and return it by dusk. Most of what you read is dry: permit records, spell logs, old measurements. Now and then, you find something that is not.", "name": "Metron Archive Reader", "tags": ["Secluded", "Bureaucratic", "Academic"]}]}, "public_id": "75de2847-eb42-55d2-83d0-12f96d35d351", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": false, "code": "weeping_sailor", "description": null, "kind": "background", "name": "Sailor", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "locked_note": "Details to be unlocked after Eastern Point of Light is re_established", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": []}, "public_id": "28623b63-e112-5399-9338-a47725093c5e", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_scribe", "description": "Variants: Weather-seer's Recorder, Inquisition Clerk", "kind": "background", "name": "Scribe", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You joined the oracular college as a recorder, since you wanted something to measure while you worked out who you were. Each day you log the rain's strength, the wind, and the color of the clouds, then compare them against the pattern the seers expect. Most days the pages match. You learned that the work is about patience and precision, and that a single missed entry can undo a decade of forecasts. Emberwatch captains and farmers wait on your ledgers, even if they never learn your name.", "name": "Weather-seer's Recorder", "tags": ["Secluded", "Bureaucratic", "Academic"]}, {"blurb": "You took a clerk's post with the Inquisition, hoping to learn the city's laws and find out whether the pool was written down anywhere. You file the Laws of the Light, carry sealed documents between offices, and check the punch-marks on the city's coin. Every page is numbered and every seal is logged. You learned how an accusation travels from a whisper to a ruling, and that a careful clerk is trusted with more than they expect.\n###", "name": "Inquisition Clerk", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Martial"]}]}, "public_id": "054119e9-5c78-5c64-8871-37a2b06321a5", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_soldier", "description": "Variants: Emberwatch Road-company, Frostmarch Recruit", "kind": "background", "name": "Soldier", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You enlisted with a company founded under Leander, because you wanted to see more than the city's walls. Your company trained together, ate together, and spent its days helping to light the outer roads. You hauled lamp-oil, raised posts, and set blessed lanterns at the road's edge, so that travelers would have a trail of warm light to follow. You learned that road-lighting is as much about teamwork as courage: one person holds the ladder, another trims the wick, a third watches the dark. The company became the closest thing you had to a family, and its jokes, rituals, and nicknames are the first memories you can call your own.", "name": "Emberwatch Road-company", "tags": ["Wilderness", "Communal", "Martial"]}, {"blurb": "You requested a place at the Frostmarch's Quarters, drawn to the hardest training the city offers. You have never left Lightfall's boundary. Your days are drills, formation work, and endless sparring against dummies carved with halos, preparing you for the fallen angels beyond the Frostmarch. The instructors talk about the north, and you listen as though it were a story you half remember. The routine is strict, and you are good at it. What you cannot shake is the feeling that you have fought something like this before.", "name": "Frostmarch Recruit", "tags": ["Secluded", "Bureaucratic", "Martial"]}]}, "public_id": "69657933-30d4-514c-ba8d-2e2d5fc43985", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_wayfarer", "description": "Variants: Ruin-delver, Laborer's Lodger", "kind": "background", "name": "Wayfarer", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You went into the old ruins on your own, hoping they would explain what you are. You scavenged for coin and clues, without license, trading relics and rumors with whoever was paying that season. You learned to read a collapsed corridor, test a floor before trusting it, and move quietly through places that have been dead since before the Age of Heroes. Automa, forgotten gods, and the floating ring all leave faint traces in the stone. Most delvers sell what they find. You keep a few pieces, just in case.", "name": "Ruin-delver", "tags": ["Wilderness", "Anarchic", "Academic"]}, {"blurb": "You rented a bed in a laborer neighborhood for a season and stayed because nobody expected you to explain yourself. You helped where you were needed: hauling, cooking, patching roofs, minding children while their parents worked. The neighbors taught you the price of bread, whose word can be trusted, and how to share a pot among twelve. You never quite belonged, but you were never turned away, and that was worth more than you expected.", "name": "Laborer's Lodger", "tags": ["Urban", "Communal", "Labor"]}]}, "public_id": "7c8a9eea-8672-5ce5-8247-50c578a434f2", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
{"conflict": ["kind", "code"], "refs": {"introduced_by_region_id": {"public_id": "72e0eb59-0562-508a-ac42-9b26e24a7b14", "table": "regions"}}, "row": {"available": true, "code": "weeping_familiar_trainer", "description": "Variants: Metron Familiar Warden", "kind": "background", "name": "Familiar Trainer", "payload": {"edition": "weeping", "source": "Codex/Backgrounds.md", "variants": [{"blurb": "You asked the Mage's Guild how a person comes to keep a familiar, and they handed you a registry book and a cage of very patient ravens. The Metron licenses every bonded creature in Lightfall, so you learned the forms, the temperament tests, and the careful rituals of introduction. Wardens match small spirits to willing animals, then train both to behave in a crowded city. A familiar that misbehaves is the Measure's problem, and so is its trainer.", "name": "Metron Familiar Warden", "tags": ["Urban", "Bureaucratic", "Academic"]}]}, "public_id": "32a4f9da-ffea-54df-9cc9-5be50d675314", "retired": false}, "table": "creation_catalog"}
